      "title": "Conceptual MCQ - Navya-Nyāya Definition of Vyāpti)",
      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In the *Vyāpti-pañcaka* section of the *Anumānakhaṇḍa* of Gaṅgeśa Upādhyāya’s *Tattvacintāmaṇi*, five preliminary definitions of *vyāpti* (invariable concomitance) are formulated and successively rejected. Consider the first tentative definition:\n$$\\text{Vyāpti} = \\text{sādhyābhāvavad-avṛttitvam} \\quad (\\text{non-occurrence of the probans in the loci of the absence of the probandum}).$$\n\nWhy does Gaṅgeśa reject this definition as suffering from the defect of *avyāpti* (under-extension / failure to cover legitimate inferences)?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Because in valid *kevalānvayi* inferences such as *\"This is nameable because it is knowable\"* (*idaṁ vācyaṁ prameyatvāt*), the probandum (*vācyatva*) is universally present (*sarvavṛtti*), rendering *sādhyābhāva* (the absence of the probandum) strictly unexemplified (*aprasiddha*), so the locus of its absence cannot be identified.",
        "B": "Because in standard *anvaya-vyatireki* inferences such as *\"The mountain has fire because it has smoke\"*, the absence of fire exists in the lake, but smoke also exists in the lake through the relation of inherence (*samavāya*), causing over-extension (*ativyāpti*).",
        "C": "Because the definition fails to incorporate *avacchedaka* (limitor) technology, thereby allowing a counter-instance where fire exists in a kitchen as kitchen-fire (*mahānasīya-vahni*), rendering the absence of hill-fire present even on the smoky mountain.",
        "D": "Because the relation determining locus-hood (*vṛttitā-niyāmaka-sambandha*) is not specified as contact (*saṁyoga*), thereby confusing mental absence (*buddhīya-abhāva*) with ontological absence (*vastu-svarūpa-abhāva*).\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) Because in valid kevalānvayi inferences such as \"This is nameable because it is knowable\" (idaṁ vācyaṁ prameyatvāt), the probandum (vācyatva) is universally present (sarvavṛtti), rendering sādhyābhāva (the absence of the probandum) strictly unexemplified (aprasiddha), so the locus of its absence cannot be identified.",
      "grounding": "In Gaṅgeśa’s *Tattvacintāmaṇi* (Anumānakhaṇḍa, *Vyāptivāda*), the first definition evaluated is *sādhyābhāvavad-avṛttitvam* (the non-existence of the *hetu* in that which possesses the absence of the *sādhya*). Gaṅgeśa demonstrates that this definition breaks down fatally in the case of *kevalānvayi-anumāna* (inferences with a universally positive probandum). In an inference like *idaṁ vācyaṁ jñeyatvāt* or *idaṁ vācyaṁ prameyatvāt*, every knowable or existent entity in the Nyāya-Vaiśeṣika ontology is nameable (*vācya*). Consequently, there is no entity anywhere in the cosmos where the absence of nameability (*vācyatvābhāva*) exists. The absence of the *sādhya* is entirely unexemplified (*aprasiddha* / *alīka*). If *sādhyābhāva* does not exist, then *sādhyābhāvavat* (the locus possessing such absence) is a null set, and one cannot determine the non-occurrence (*avṛttitvam*) of the *hetu* therein. Thus, a definition that requires locating *sādhyābhāva* fails to cover valid *kevalānvayi* inferences, suffering from the cardinal logical fault of *avyāpti*. This necessitates Gaṅgeśa's eventual formulation of the *Siddhānta-lakṣaṇa*: *pratiyogyanadhikaraṇa-kāpīkṣaṇāvacchinna-sādhyasamānādhikaraṇa...* / *hetusamānādhikaraṇa-virahāpratiyogitā-avacchedakāvacchinna-sādhyasāmānādhikaraṇyaṁ vyāptiḥ*.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option B is incorrect**: In Nyāya, the relation by which the *hetu* occurs is implicitly or explicitly governed by the relation of occurrence (*hetutāvacchedaka-sambandha*). Smoke does not occur in a lake by inherence in any standard counter-argument; rather, the standard issue with inherence is that smoke inheres only in its parts (smoke-particles), not in the lake or the mountain. Moreover, B misidentifies the defect as *ativyāpti* rather than the foundational *avyāpti* regarding *kevalānvayi*.\n- **Option C is incorrect**: While the problem of distinguishing generic absence (*sāmānyābhāva*) from specific absence (*viśeṣābhāva*, such as *mahānasīyavahnyabhāva*) is a central preoccupation in Navya-Nyāya, it is not the reason Gaṅgeśa rejects the very first formulation in the *Vyāpti-pañcaka*. That specific challenge is treated under *vyadhikaraṇa-dharmāvacchinnābhāva* and the qualification of *sādhyatāvacchedaka-sambandha*.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: Nyāya rigorously rejects purely \"mental absences\" (*buddhīya-abhāva*) in its realist epistemology; absences (*abhāva*) are objective ontological padārthas. This formulation uses pseudo-Navya terminology to mask an irrelevant metaphysical thesis.\n\n---",
