      "title": "Conceptual MCQ - Epistemic Status of Nirvikalpaka Pratyakṣa: Nyāya vs. Buddhism)",
      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "The transition from indeterminate perception (*nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa*) to determinate perception (*savikalpaka-pratyakṣa*) constitutes a critical battleground between the Buddhist epistemologists (Diṅnāga and Dharmakīrti) and the Naiyāyikas (from Jayanta Bhaṭṭa to Gaṅgeśa). \n\nWhich of the following propositions accurately reflects the **Navya-Nyāya** doctrine regarding the cognitive accessibility and structure of *nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa*?",
      "options": {
        "A": "*Nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa* is an internally luminous, directly experienced state (*svasaṁvedana*), which is subsequently structured into subject-predicate relations by verbal memory.",
        "B": "*Nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa* is entirely beyond direct introspective perception (*atīndriya* / *anupalabhya*); it is not apprehended by reflective apperception (*anuvyavasāya*), but is posited strictly as a necessary logical/epistemic inference (*anumeya*), because a qualified cognition (*savikalpaka-viśiṣṭa-jñāna*) logically presupposes the prior apprehension of the qualifier (*viśeṣaṇa*) without qualification.",
        "C": "*Nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa* is identical with the Buddhist *svalakṣaṇa*, apprehending pure unnameable efficiency (*arthakriyāsāmarthya*), which is distorted as soon as *jāti* (universal) is superimposed.",
        "D": "*Nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa* grasps only the bare substance (*dravya-mātra*), while the universal (*sāmānya*) and the inherence relation (*samavāya*) are supplied by the internal organ (*manas*) as mental fictions.\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B) Nirvikalpaka-pratyakṣa is entirely beyond direct introspective perception (atīndriya / anupalabhya); it is not apprehended by reflective apperception (anuvyavasāya), but is posited strictly as a necessary logical/epistemic inference (anumeya), because a qualified cognition (savikalpaka-viśiṣṭa-jñāna) logically presupposes the prior apprehension of the qualifier (viśeṣaṇa) without qualification.",
      "grounding": "In Navya-Nyāya, particularly in Gaṅgeśa's *Tattvacintāmaṇi* (Pratyakṣakhaṇḍa, *Nirvikalpakavāda*) and Annambhaṭṭa's *Tarkasaṁgraha-Dīpikā*:\n- *Nirvikalpaka* is defined as: *vaiśiṣṭyānavagāhi jñānam* (cognition that does not grasp any relational qualification or relational structure between substantive, qualifier, and relation: *viśeṣya-viśeṣaṇa-sambandhānavagāhi*).\n- Because all introspective apperception (*anuvyavasāya*) takes a structured, propositional form (e.g., *\"I know the pot\"*—*ghaṭam ahaṁ jānāmi*, where \"pot-ness\" qualifies the pot), *anuvyavasāya* can only register *savikalpaka* cognition. It can never turn backwards to catch *nirvikalpaka* cognition in the act.\n- Therefore, how is *nirvikalpaka* proven to exist? It is established by inference (*anumāna*):\n  $$\\text{Viśiṣṭa-buddhiḥ viśeṣaṇa-jñāna-janyā, viśiṣṭa-buddhitvāt, daṇḍīti-buddhivat.}$$\n  (\"Every cognition of a qualified entity is generated by the prior cognition of the qualifier, because it is a qualified cognition, just like the cognition 'a man with a staff' presupposes the prior cognition of the staff\").\n  Before one can cognize \"This is a pot\" (*ayam ghaṭaḥ*, where pot-ness is the qualifier), one must have had an unconstructed cognition of \"pot-ness\" and \"the object\" in isolation, free of relational synthesis. That primary, unstructured cognitive stage is *nirvikalpaka*. It is real, objective, and valid, but strictly ungraspable by introspection (*atīndriya*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: Positing *svasaṁvedana* (self-intimating/self-conscious nature of cognition) is the hallmark of Yogācāra-Sautrāntika Buddhism and Prabhākara Mīmāṁsā, which Nyāya uncompromisingly rejects. For Nyāya, a cognition cannot cognize itself; it is cognized only by a subsequent act of apperception (*anuvyavasāya*).\n- **Option C is incorrect**: This is the Buddhist view of Dharmakīrti (*Nyāyabindu*: *pratyakṣaṁ kalpanāpoḍham abhrāntam*). For the Buddhist, the universal (*sāmānya*) is an unreal mental construct (*vikalpa* / *apoha*). For the Naiyāyika, the universal is an eternal, objective ontological reality (*nityam ekam anekānugatam*), grasped objectively even in *nirvikalpaka*.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: In Nyāya realism, neither *sāmānya* nor *samavāya* is a mental fiction. Both exist out there in the mind-independent world and are directly captured in reality by sensory contact.\n\n---",
