      "grounding": "1. **Prābhākara Mīmāṁsā (Akhyātivāda)**: In *Prakaraṇapañcikā*, Prabhākara asserts that error is fundamentally omission, not commission—*bhedāgraha* (non-apprehension of the difference) between two distinct cognitive events: the perception of \"this\" (*purovartin*) and the memory of \"silver\" (*smṛti*). Because all knowledge is intrinsically valid (*svataḥ-pramāṇa*), there is no misapprehension of an unreal entity, but a failure of discrimination (**P-2-ii**).\n2. **Advaita Vedānta (Anirvacanīyakhyāti)**: In Śaṅkara’s *Adhyāsabhāṣya* and Prakāśātman’s *Pañcapādikā-Vivarana*, the apparent silver cannot be real (*sat*), because it is sublated by subsequent knowledge; nor can it be unreal (*asat*), because it is visually experienced; nor can it be both, because that violates non-contradiction. Hence, it is an inexplicable, apparent creation (*anirvacanīya-khyāti*) produced by *tūlāvidyā* (**Q-3-i**).\n3. **Nyāya (Anyathākhyātivāda)**: In Vātsyāyana’s *Nyāyabhāṣya* and Gaṅgeśa’s *Tattvacintāmaṇi*, error is apprehension of an object as otherwise (*anyathā-prakāraka*). The silver exists elsewhere (e.g., in a shop); through memory-revival functioning as an extraordinary perceptual connection (*jñānalakṣaṇa-sannikarṣa*), that real elsewhere-silver is cognized as qualifying the real shell presented before the eyes (**R-1-iii**).\n4. **Mādhyamika Buddhism (Asatkhyātivāda)**: In Mādhyamika dialectic (e.g., Candrakīrti’s *Prasannapadā*), error consists in the projection of intrinsic existence (*svabhāva*) onto phenomena that are inherently empty (*śūnya*) and non-existent as intrinsic realities (**S-4-iv**).",
