      "question": "Within the Sarvāstivāda stream of Early Buddhism, the **Vaibhāṣika** and **Sautrāntika** schools fundamentally split over the epistemological accessibility of the external world.\n\nWhich of the following statements correctly captures the precise epistemic reason why the **Sautrāntika** insists on **Bāhyānumeyavāda** (the indirect, inferential reality of external objects), and how it rejects the Vaibhāṣika doctrine of direct perception (**Bāhyapratyakṣavāda**)?",
      "options": {
        "A": "The Sautrāntika holds that external objects are mere illusions produced by karma, so they can only be inferred through scriptures.",
        "B": "Because according to *Kṣaṇikavāda*, the external object that causes a perception perishes in the very moment of its causal operation ($t_1$); hence, by the time the sensory consciousness arises in the subsequent moment ($t_2$), the object no longer exists, meaning consciousness can only perceive its own internalized cognitive imprint/form (*jñānākāra*), from which the antecedent existence of the external object is inferred as its cause.",
        "C": "Because the Sautrāntika rejects the authority of the *Abhidharma-mahāvibhāṣā* and accepts only the *Vinaya Piṭaka*, which prohibits the direct visual inspection of material forms.",
        "D": "Because external objects consist of indivisible partless atoms, and partless atoms are by definition beyond the threshold of sensory apprehension (*atīndriya*).\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B) Because according to Kṣaṇikavāda, the external object that causes a perception perishes in the very moment of its causal operation ($t_1$); hence, by the time the sensory consciousness arises in the subsequent moment ($t_2$), the object no longer exists, meaning consciousness can only perceive its own internalized cognitive imprint/form (jñānākāra), from which the antecedent existence of the external object is inferred as its cause.",
      "grounding": "In Vasubandhu’s *Abhidharmakośabhāṣya* (Chapter 1 & 9) and Mādhavācārya’s *Sarvadarśanasaṁgraha* (*Bauddhadarśanam*):\n- **Vaibhāṣika View (Bāhyapratyakṣavāda)**: The Vaibhāṣikas (following the *Mahāvibhāṣā*) hold that external objects exist substantially (*dravyasat*) across all three times (past, present, future: *sarvam asti*) and are directly grasped by sensory perception without the mediation of an internal cognitive image (*nirākāra-jñāna-vāda*).\n- **Sautrāntika Critique and Bāhyānumeyavāda**:\n  1. The Sautrāntika enforces the strict Buddhist law of momentariness (*kṣaṇikavāda*) and causality. A cause must temporally precede its effect.\n  2. The external object (e.g., a blue flower) acts as the objective condition (*ālambana-pratyaya*) at moment $t_1$ to generate the visual consciousness (*cakṣur-vijñāna*).\n  3. But according to universal momentariness, the external blue object perishes at the very end of $t_1$.\n  4. The sensory consciousness arises at moment $t_2$. At $t_2$, the external object is already dead and gone!\n  5. How can consciousness directly perceive an object that does not exist at the moment consciousness occurs? Direct contact (*pratyakṣa*) with a non-existent entity is impossible.\n  6. Therefore, what consciousness directly experiences at $t_2$ is the cognitive form or impression (*ākāra*) stamped onto consciousness by the external object before it vanished (*sākāra-jñāna-vāda*).\n  7. From this distinct cognitive form (*ākāra-viśeṣa*), we infer the prior existence of the external object as its material cause (*kāraṇa-liṅgaka-bāhyārtha-anumāna*). Hence: *Bāhyānumeyavāda*.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: The Sautrāntika is a realist school; it does *not* claim that external objects are mere illusions or mind-only creations (that is the Yogācāra idealist stance).\n- **Option C is incorrect**: Sautrāntikas are named after the *Sūtra Piṭaka* (not *Vinaya*), and their epistemic theories are derived from rigorous logical analysis of causality and momentariness, not monastic taboos.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: While both schools analyze matter into atoms, the specific epistemic divergence of *Bāhyānumeyavāda* turns strictly upon the temporal gap between the causal moment ($t_1$) and the effect moment ($t_2$) in light of *kṣaṇikavāda*.\n\n---",
