      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In the *Triṁśikā-kārikā* and *Madhyāntavibhāga*, Vasubandhu and Maitreyanātha expound the foundational metaphysics of Yogācāra (Vijñānavāda). \n\nExamine the following four statements regarding the doctrine of the **Three Natures (Trisvabhāva)** and the **Storehouse Consciousness (Ālayavijñāna)**:\n\nI. The *Parikalpita-svabhāva* (imagined nature) refers to the false imputation of the subject-object duality (*grāhya-grāhaka-bhāva*) and external mind-independent entities onto the continuous flow of consciousness.  \nII. The *Paratantra-svabhāva* (dependent nature) represents the causal network of momentary conscious events (*cittacaittāḥ*) arising through dependent origination (*pratītyasamutpāda*), functioning as the locus of both delusion and purification.  \nIII. The *Pariniṣpanna-svabhāva* (perfected nature) is the complete and permanent absence of the imagined duality (*parikalpita*) within the dependent nature (*paratantra*), realizing Suchness (*tathatā*).  \nIV. The *Ālayavijñāna* is an eternal, unchanging, transcendental Self (*ātman*) identical with the Advaitic Brahman, which permanently witnesses the six active sense-consciousnesses (*pravṛtti-vijñāna*).\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I, II, and III only",
        "B": "I and IV only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, and III only",
      "grounding": "In Vasubandhu’s *Triṁśikā-vijñaptimātratāsiddhi* (verses 20–22):\n- **Statement I is correct**: *Yena yena vikalpena yad yad vastu vikalpyate, parikalpita evāsau svabhāvo na sa vidyate* (Kārikā 20). The imagined nature (*parikalpita*) is the conceptual reification of phenomenal reality into external objects to be grasped (*grāhya*) and an internal subject that grasps (*grāhaka*). It is purely nominal, lacking intrinsic reality.\n- **Statement II is correct**: *Paratantra-svabhāvas tu vikalpaḥ pratyayodbhavaḥ* (Kārikā 21). The dependent nature is the dynamic stream of causally conditioned mental phenomena (*vikalpa* / cognitive events) emerging from underlying karmic seeds (*bīja*). It is called dependent (*para-tantra*) because it depends on antecedent causal conditions (*pratyaya*).\n- **Statement III is correct**: *Niṣpannas tasya pūrveṇa sadā rahitatā tu yā* (Kārikā 21). The perfected nature (*pariniṣpanna*) is nothing other than the *paratantra* completely and eternally emptied of the *parikalpita* (the subject-object bifurcation). It is the non-dual ultimate reality (*dharmatā* / *tathatā*).\n- **Statement IV is INCORRECT**: In Yogācāra, the *Ālayavijñāna* (storehouse consciousness) is emphatically **NOT** an eternal, unchanging Self (*ātman*)! Vasubandhu explicitly states in *Triṁśikā* (verse 4):\n  $$\\text{\"tac ca vartate srotasaughavat\"}$$\n  (\"And that [ālayavijñāna] flows continuously like a raging river torrent\").\n  The *Ālayavijñāna* is a dynamic, rapidly changing stream of momentary cognitive point-instants carrying karmic seeds (*sarva-bījakam*). At the attainment of Arhatship/Enlightenment (*āśraya-parāvṛtti*—the fundamental revolution of the basis), the stream of *ālayavijñāna* is completely transformed and extinguished. Equating it with an eternal Advaitic *Ātman* is a cardinal error in Buddhist scholarship.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since Statement IV commits the fatal error of eternalizing the *Ālayavijñāna* into an unchanging *Ātman* (violating the core Buddhist axiom of *Anātmavāda*), any option containing IV (B, C, and D) is definitively eliminated.\n\n---",
