      "title": "Conceptual MCQ - Mādhyamika Catuṣkoṭi on Causation)",
      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In the opening chapter of the *Mūlamadhyamakakārikā* (*Pratyayaparīkṣā*, MMK 1.1), Nāgārjuna presents the celebrated **Catuṣkoṭi** (fourfold tetralemma) negating all four possible ontological permutations of causal origination:\n$$\\text{\"na svato nāpi parato na dvābhyāṁ nāpy ahetutaḥ |}$$\n$$\\text{utpannā jātu vidyante bhāvāḥ kvacana kecana ||\"}$$\n\nWhich of the following correctly pairs each of the four rejected positions (*koṭis*) with the specific philosophical school holding that thesis, and Nāgārjuna's exact dialectical refutation of it?",
      "options": {
        "A": "1. Self-origination (*svataḥ*): Held by Sāṁkhya (*Satkāryavāda*); refuted because if the effect already exists within the cause, its production is redundant and meaningless (*janma-vaiyarthyam*), and production would never cease.  \n2. Origination from another (*parataḥ*): Held by Nyāya-Vaiśeṣika (*Asatkāryavāda*); refuted because if an effect could be produced from an entirely other/alien cause, then thick darkness could be produced from a blazing lamp.  \n3. Origination from both (*dvābhyām*): Held by Jaina metaphysics (*Anekāntavāda*); refuted because it merely combines the absurdities and contradictions of both self-origination and other-origination.  \n4. Uncaused origination (*ahetutaḥ*): Held by Cārvāka / Svabhāvavāda; refuted because if things were uncaused, effects would arise anywhere, at any time, without restriction, or never arise at all.",
        "B": "1. Self-origination: Held by Nyāya; refuted because atoms cannot combine.  \n2. Origination from another: Held by Sāṁkhya; refuted because Prakṛti is blind.  \n3. Origination from both: Held by Advaita; refuted because Brahman is non-dual.  \n4. Uncaused origination: Held by Buddhism; refuted because Buddha taught karma.",
        "C": "1. Self-origination: Held by Yogācāra; refuted because mind is empty.  \n2. Origination from another: Held by Cārvāka; refuted because perception is flawed.  \n3. Origination from both: Held by Vaibhāṣika; refuted because past and future exist.  \n4. Uncaused origination: Held by Jaimini; refuted because Vedas are eternal.",
        "D": "1. Self-origination: Held by Mīmāṁsā; refuted by Apurva.  \n2. Origination from another: Held by Dvaita; refuted by Taratamya.  \n3. Origination from both: Held by Kashmir Śaivism; refuted by Spanda.  \n4. Uncaused origination: Held by Cārvāka; refuted by Lokāyata.\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) 1. Self-origination (svataḥ): Held by Sāṁkhya (Satkāryavāda); refuted because if the effect already exists within the cause, its production is redundant and meaningless (janma-vaiyarthyam), and production would never cease. 2. Origination from another (parataḥ): Held by Nyāya-Vaiśeṣika (Asatkāryavāda); refuted because if an effect could be produced from an entirely other/alien cause, then thick darkness could be produced from a blazing lamp. 3. Origination from both (dvābhyām): Held by Jaina metaphysics (Anekāntavāda); refuted because it merely combines the absurdities and contradictions of both self-origination and other-origination. 4. Uncaused origination (ahetutaḥ): Held by Cārvāka / Svabhāvavāda; refuted because if things were uncaused, effects would arise anywhere, at any time, without restriction, or never arise at all.",
      "grounding": "In Candrakīrti’s *Prasannapadā* on MMK 1.1:\n1. **First Koṭi (*na svataḥ* - Refutation of Sāṁkhya)**: If the jar is identical with the clay (*tādātmya*), the clay is already the jar. The jar is already existent. Producing what is already existent is totally futile (*utpannasya punar utpādo niṣprayojanaḥ*). Moreover, if an existent entity is produced, it could never stop being produced.\n2. **Second Koṭi (*na parataḥ* - Refutation of Nyāya-Vaiśeṣika and Buddhist Sarvāstivāda)**: If the effect is utterly different (*atyanta-bhinna*) from the cause, then any other entity could produce any other effect—*sarvaḥ sarvata utpadyeta* (flames could produce pitch darkness).\n3. **Third Koṭi (*na dvābhyām* - Refutation of Jainism)**: Jainism holds that the effect is partly identical with and partly different from the cause (*bhedābheda*). Nāgārjuna points out that combining two flawed positions does not cure their defects; it simply doubles the logical faults (*ubhayapakṣa-bhaviṣyad-doṣa-prasādhyam*).\n4. **Fourth Koṭi (*nāpy ahetutaḥ* - Refutation of Accidentalism/Svabhāvavāda/Cārvāka)**: If effects arose without cause, then all intentional human action (*kārya-kāraṇa-pravṛtti*) would become meaningless, and anything could arise out of nothing at any place and time (*ahetutve sarvaṁ sarvadā sambhavet*).\n5. **Conclusion**: Since an entity cannot arise from itself, nor from another, nor from both, nor without a cause, production (*utpāda*) is an unconstructed conceptual fiction (*prapañca*). In ultimate truth (*paramārtha*), nothing is ever born (*Ajātivāda*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Options B, C, and D misattribute the four philosophical schools behind the *koṭis* (e.g., claiming Nyāya holds self-origination or Buddhism holds uncaused origination), displaying complete confusion regarding the classic Indian doxographical mapping of MMK 1.1.\n\n---",
