      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In Jaina epistemology (*Tattvārthasūtra* 1.33 of Umāsvāti and *Nyāyāvatāra* of Siddhasena Divākara), **Nayavāda** (the doctrine of partial viewpoints) systematizes cognitive perspectives into seven distinct *nayas*.\n\nEvaluate the following statements regarding the seven *nayas*:\n\nI. The *Dravyārthika-nayas* (substantial standpoints) comprise *Naigama* (teleological/common-sense), *Saṁgraha* (universalizing/class), and *Vyavahāra* (empirical/practical classification).  \nII. The *Paryāyārthika-nayas* (modal standpoints) prioritize the changing, temporal, and differentiated aspects of reality, beginning with *Ṛjusūtra-naya*, which restricts reality solely to the present instantaneous moment.  \nIII. *Śabda-naya* accepts that synonymous words (like *Indra*, *Śakra*, and *Purandara*) denote entirely different ontological objects based on their distinct etymological roots.  \nIV. *Evambhūta-naya* is the most restrictive of all nayas, asserting that a name can only be truthfully applied to an object when the object is actively performing the exact functional activity signified by that name (e.g., calling someone *pūjaka* only while they are in the active physical act of worship).\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I, II, and IV only",
        "B": "I and III only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, and IV only",
      "grounding": "In Umāsvāti’s *Tattvārthasūtra* (1.33: *naigama-saṁgraha-vyavahāra-ṛjusūtra-śabda-samabhirūḍha-evambhūtā nayāḥ*) and Pūjyapāda’s *Sarvārthasiddhi*:\n- **Statement I is correct**: The first three nayas are *Dravyārthika* because they apprehend the underlying, enduring substance (*dravya*). *Naigama* considers purpose/end-result; *Saṁgraha* groups particulars under general universals; *Vyavahāra* divides the universals into pragmatic sub-classes.\n- **Statement II is correct**: The remaining four are *Paryāyārthika* because they attend to modes/states (*paryāya*). *Ṛjusūtra* (the \"straight-thread\" view) cuts off past and future, apprehending only the currently existing, present-moment reality (very close to the Buddhist view).\n- **Statement III is INCORRECT**: Statement III conflates *Śabda-naya* with *Samabhirūḍha-naya*. \n  - *Śabda-naya* (the verbal standpoint) recognizes grammatical correctness, tense, gender, and case, but **accepts synonyms** as referring to the same object (e.g., *Indra*, *Śakra*, and *Purandara* refer to the same king of gods).\n  - It is **Samabhirūḍha-naya** (the etymological/conventional standpoint) that rejects synonymy, asserting that because their etymological derivations differ (*Indra* = all-powerful, *Śakra* = capable, *Purandara* = destroyer of cities), each word denotes a distinct nuance or phase of the object.\n- **Statement IV is correct**: *Evambhūta-naya* (\"the 'such-actuated' standpoint\") is the ultimate, most rigorous linguistic specialization. It insists that an object can only bear a title when the activity denoted by that title is actively occurring. One is a \"cook\" (*pācaka*) only while actively stirring the pot, not while asleep.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since Statement III incorrectly assigns the rejection of synonyms to *Śabda-naya* rather than *Samabhirūḍha-naya*, any option containing III (B, C, and D) is invalid.\n\n---",
