      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B) Purandara accepts lokaprasiddha-anumāna (inferences concerning observable, empirical matters of everyday life, such as inferring fire from smoke), because they are verifiable by direct perception, but strictly rejects alaukika- / paraloka-sādhaka-anumāna (inferences that posit unobservable, transcendent entities like Īśvara, Adṛṣṭa, Atman, or the afterlife), because such transcendent inferences transcend the horizon of empirical verification.",
      "grounding": "In Kamalaśīla’s *Tattvasaṁgrahapañjikā* (commentary on verse 1482):\n$$\\text{\"purandaras tv āha—loka-prasiddham anumānaṁ cārvākair apīṣyata eva,}$$\n$$\\text{yat tu kaiścid laukikam mārga-matītya anumānam ucyate, tan niṣidhyate.\"}$$\n(\"Purandara, however, says: Inferences well-known in ordinary worldly life are indeed accepted even by the Cārvākas; but that inference which is declared by certain philosophers by transcending the worldly empirical realm—that alone is rejected.\")\n\nPurandara recognized that a total, dogmatic rejection of all inference would render everyday human life impossible: one could not even cook food (inferring that fire cooks), walk on a street (inferring the ground will hold), or communicate. \nTherefore, Purandara formulated a crucial epistemological demarcation:\n1. **Loka-prasiddha / Utpannapratīti Anumāna**: Inferences whose conclusions can, in principle, be directly confirmed by subsequent perception (e.g., inferring fire from smoke on a nearby hill and then walking up to see the fire). This practical, empirically bounded inference is admitted.\n2. **Atīndriya / Paraloka Anumāna**: Inferences constructed by theologians and metaphysicians to establish unobservable entities that can *never* be checked or confirmed by perception (such as an unembodied Soul, Karma/Adṛṣṭa, Heaven, Hell, or an eternal Creator God). This metaphysical leap into the transcendental void is categorically rejected as illegitimate and pseudo-logical.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: Cārvāka never accepts the authority of the Vedas or transcendent entities; Purandara's whole goal was to dismantle transcendent inferences while salvaging worldly common-sense inference.\n- **Option C is incorrect**: Classical Indian materialists did not bifurcate logic into empirical vs. pure mathematical formalism in the modern Cartesian sense.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: This trivializes Purandara’s serious epistemological distinction between empirical and transcendent domains into arbitrary political decree.\n\n---",
