      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In *Sāṁkhyakārikā* (Kārikā 9), Īśvarakṛṣṇa formulates five classic arguments to establish *Satkāryavāda* (the pre-existence of the effect in the material cause prior to its production):\n$$\\text{\"asad-akaraṇāt upādāna-grahaṇāt sarva-sambhavābhāvāt}$$\n$$\\text{śaktasya śakya-karaṇāt kāraṇa-bhāvāc ca sat-kāryam.\"}$$\n\nConsider the argument from **śaktasya śakya-karaṇāt** (\"a potent cause produces only that for which it is potent\"). How does this specific argument structurally undermine the Nyāya-Vaiśeṣika doctrine of *Asatkāryavāda* (*Ārambhavāda*), according to the Sāṁkhya commentators (Vācaspati Miśra in *Tattvakaumudī* and Gauḍapāda)?",
