      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) If the effect were entirely non-existent (asat) prior to production, the causal potency (śakti) inherent in the cause could have no ontological relation (sambandha) with that non-entity, because a real potency cannot relate to an absolute void; thus, oil could as easily be pressed from sand as from sesame seeds.",
      "grounding": "In Īśvarakṛṣṇa’s *Sāṁkhyakārikā* 9 and Vācaspati Miśra’s *Sāṁkhyatattvakaumudī*, the argument *śaktasya śakya-karaṇāt* targets the ontological relationality of causal power. \n\nThe Naiyāyika claims *Asatkāryavāda*—that the effect is an absolute non-existence before its causal generation (*prāgabhāva-pratiyogi*). But when the Naiyāyika is asked why oil is produced exclusively from sesame seeds and never from sand, the Naiyāyika replies that sesame seeds possess a specific potency (*śakti*) directed toward oil, which sand lacks. \n\nVācaspati Miśra uncovers the fatal contradiction in this Nyāya defense:\nPotency (*śakti*) is inherently relational; it is power *toward* something (*viṣaya* / *śakya*). Power cannot exist in a vacuum without an object to which it is directed. If the effect (oil) is non-existent (*asat*) prior to production, it is an absolute nothingness (*alīka*, like a sky-flower). A real causal potency existing in the seeds cannot enter into any ontological relation (*sambandha*)—neither contact (*saṁyoga*) nor inherence (*samavāya*)—with an absolute non-entity! If potency could produce an effect with which it has no relation, then anything could produce everything (*sarva-sambhavaprasaṅga*). Therefore, the effect must already exist in a latent, unmanifested form (*sūkṣma / avyakta rūpa*) within the cause, serving as the objective correlate of the cause’s active potency. Causal production is thus not creation *ex nihilo* (*ārambha*), but merely manifestation (*tirobhāva-āvirbhāva* / *pariṇāma*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option B is incorrect**: Classical Sāṁkhya (*Nirīśvara-Sāṁkhya*) explicitly rejects Īśvara as an active creator (*kāraṇa*); causality is entirely naturalistic, driven by the inherent teleology of *Prakṛti* for the sake of the *Puruṣa* (*puruṣārtha*).\n- **Option C is incorrect**: Sāṁkhya never asserts that the effect is produced through the destruction of the cause; that is the Buddhist view of *kṣaṇikatvavāda* / *pratītyasamutpāda* (seed must perish for the sprout to emerge). For Sāṁkhya, the underlying *dravya* (the material substrate) is conserved eternally.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: The argument *śaktasya śakya-karaṇāt* establishes the essential identity (*tādātmya*) and continuity between cause and effect (*kāraṇa-bhāvāt*), rather than temporal alienation.\n\n---",
