      "title": "Multi-Statement MSQ - Vaiśeṣika Atomic Theory: Pīlupāka vs. Piṭharapāka)",
      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In classical Indian metaphysics, the transformation of qualities (such as color, taste, and smell) in physical substances through the action of heat/fire (*tejas*) forms the celebrated debate between the **Vaiśeṣika** doctrine of **Pīlupākavāda** and the **Nyāya** doctrine of **Piṭharapākavāda**.\n\nExamine the following statements regarding the metaphysical mechanisms of these two theories:\n\nI. According to the Vaiśeṣika doctrine of *Pīlupāka*, when an unbaked black earthen pot is baked in a kiln, the intense fire-contact (*agnisaṁyoga*) disintegrates the pot down to its ultimate constituents—the atoms (*pīlu* / *paramāṇu*); the heat then destroys the original black color in the isolated atoms and produces a red color, after which the atoms recombine via dyads (*dvyaṇuka*) and triads (*tryaṇuka*) to reconstruct a brand new red pot.  \nII. According to the Nyāya doctrine of *Piṭharapāka*, the pot (*piṭhara*) is never destroyed into its constituent atoms during baking; rather, the fire particles penetrate the porous structure (*antarpraveśa*) of the unbroken, composite pot, changing the color of the whole simultaneously with its parts.  \nIII. The Vaiśeṣika admits *Pīlupāka* because it maintains that two mutually contradictory qualities (blackness and redness) can co-exist within the same composite substance (*avayavin*) provided they occupy different atomic layers.  \nIV. The debate directly reflects their underlying ontology of the whole (*avayavin*): Nyāya emphasizes the structural stability of the composite whole, whereas Vaiśeṣika prioritizes atomic autonomy and the principle that qualities of the whole are strictly caused by the preceding qualities of its ultimate parts.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I, II, and IV only",
        "B": "I and III only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, and IV only",
      "grounding": "In Praśastapāda’s *Padārthadharmasaṁgraha* (with Śrīdhara’s *Nyāyakandalī*) and Viśvanātha’s *Kārikāvalī* (verses 105–109):\n- **Statement I is correct**: *Pīlu* means atom (*paramāṇu*). The Vaiśeṣika holds that fire cannot alter the intrinsic quality of an intact composite whole without first acting on the parts. The furious contact of fire causes molecular dissolution: it destroys the conjunction of atoms forming dyads (*dvyaṇuka*), destroying the dyads, triads, and the macroscopic pot. In the bare, disunited atoms, heat destroys the previous quality (black) and produces the new quality (red). Then, driven by unseen moral destiny (*adṛṣṭa*), the atoms undergo fresh conjunction (*saṁyoga*) to rebuild dyads, triads, and eventually a new macroscopic red pot (*rakta-ghaṭa*).\n- **Statement II is correct**: *Piṭhara* means the composite pot/vessel (*avayavin*). The Naiyāyika considers the Vaiśeṣika view an absurdity that contradicts empirical recognition (*pratyabhijñā*: \"This is the very same pot I placed in the kiln\"). Nyāya argues that the pot remains structurally intact; fire-atoms (*tejaḥ-paramāṇu*) possess extreme subtlety and velocity, entering into the interstitial spaces/pores of the clay and baking the pot as an unbroken whole (*piṭharapāka*).\n- **Statement III is INCORRECT**: Vaiśeṣika does **NOT** believe that contradictory qualities can co-exist in the same substance! In fact, the absolute impossibility of contradictory qualities (such as blackness and redness) co-existing in the same substrate is the *primary reason* Vaiśeṣika insists on the complete destruction of the old black pot before the red pot can come into existence!\n- **Statement IV is correct**: The doctrine of *Pīlupāka* rigorously enforces the Vaiśeṣika causal law that qualities in an effect (*kāryaguṇa*) must be preceded by and generated from corresponding qualities in its inherent causes (*kāraṇaguṇa-pūrvaka*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since Statement III contains a blatant misrepresentation of Vaiśeṣika ontology (Vaiśeṣika strictly adheres to the law of non-contradiction regarding qualities occupying a single *dravya*), any option including III (B, C, and D) is invalid.\n\n---",
