      "question": "In the taxonomy of *Khyātivāda*, the **Yogācāra (Vijñānavāda)** school of Buddhism propounds the doctrine of **Ātmakhyātivāda**. \n\nWhat is the exact philosophical formulation of error according to *Ātmakhyātivāda*, and how is it refuted by the realists (Nyāya and Kumārila Bhaṭṭa)?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Error is the perception of an absolute non-existence (*alīka*), refuted by the realists on the grounds that nothingness cannot cast a cognitive shadow.",
        "B": "Error consists in the mind cognizing an internal cognitive form (*buddhy-ākāra* / *vijñāna-svarūpa*) as if it were an external, mind-independent physical object (*bāhyārtha*); realists refute this by arguing that if external objects did not objectively exist, the internal cognition could never possess the phenomenal form of \"out-thereness\" (*bāhyatva*), since an analogy cannot function without an authentic external archetype.",
        "C": "Error consists in mistaking one external object for another external object, refuted by realists because Yogācāra denies the existence of the mind.",
        "D": "Error is the sublation of the subject-object duality into pure void (*śūnyatā*), refuted by realists who defend the eternal soul.\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B) Error consists in the mind cognizing an internal cognitive form (buddhy-ākāra / vijñāna-svarūpa) as if it were an external, mind-independent physical object (bāhyārtha); realists refute this by arguing that if external objects did not objectively exist, the internal cognition could never possess the phenomenal form of \"out-thereness\" (bāhyatva), since an analogy cannot function without an authentic external archetype.",
      "grounding": "In Vasubandhu’s *Viṁśikā* and *Triṁśikā*, as well as Kumārila Bhaṭṭa’s *Ślokavārttika* (*Nirālambanavāda*) and Jayanta Bhaṭṭa’s *Nyāyamañjarī*:\n- **Yogācāra stance (Ātmakhyātivāda)**: Consciousness is self-luminous and diversified by beginningless subliminal impressions (*anādi-vāsanā*). There is no mind-independent external world (*na asti bāhyo 'rthaḥ*). What appears in perceptual illusion (like a dream, or the illusion of silver) is merely the internal form of cognition itself (*ātmanaḥ khyātiḥ* / *jñānākārasya khyātiḥ*). Due to ignorance, this purely internal subjective idea (*antar-jñeya-rūpa*) appears externally projected (*bahirvat avabhāsate*).\n- **Realist Refutation (Nyāya and Bhāṭṭa Mīmāṁsā)**: Kumārila and the Naiyāyikas seize upon the phrase *bahirvat* (\"as if external\"). They argue:\n  1. If an external entity (*bāhya-artha*) had never been perceived or did not exist in reality, the very concept of \"externality\" (*bāhyatva*) would be completely meaningless. One cannot say something appears \"like an external object\" unless external objects are genuinely known to exist (just as no one can say \"Devadatta looks like the son of a barren woman\").\n  2. If all cognitive appearances were purely subjective states, cognition could never display spatio-temporal restriction (*deśa-kāla-niyama*); a person would see pots and silver anywhere and everywhere at will.\n  3. Therefore, cognitive forms reflect real, mind-independent entities, proving that error cannot be reduced to subjective internal ideas projecting outward.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: The apprehension of absolute non-existence is **Asatkhyātivāda** (associated with the Mādhyamika).\n- **Option C is incorrect**: Yogācāra famously asserts the exclusive reality of consciousness (*cittamātratā*); it certainly does not deny the existence of the mind, nor is *Ātmakhyāti* about mistaking one external object for another (that is *Anyathākhyāti*).\n- **Option D is incorrect**: This conflates Yogācāra idealism with Mādhyamika dialectic.\n\n---",
