      "question": "Match the six classical Indian epistemological-metaphysical theories of error in **Column I** with their definitive philosophical propositions in **Column II**:\n\n| Column I (Khyātivāda) | Column II (Definitive Philosophical Proposition) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **P. Akhyātivāda** | 1. The object of error is an absolute non-being (*asat*), projected as being through conceptual construction (*vikalpa*). |\n| **Q. Anyathākhyātivāda** | 2. Error is purely omission: the failure to grasp the distinction between a present percept and a recalled mental trace (*smṛti-pramoṣa*). |\n| **R. Anirvacanīyakhyāti** | 3. Error is the presentation of the self/cognition's own internal form as an external independent entity (*antar-jñeyarūpaṁ tu bahirvad avabhāsate*). |\n| **S. Satkhyātivāda** | 4. Error is apprehension otherwise: a real object existing elsewhere is perceived in the present locus through extraordinary contact (*jñānalakṣaṇa*). |\n| **T. Ātmakhyātivāda** | 5. The object of error is an ontological reality; all cognitions are true because all physical objects contain particles of all other elements via quintuplication. |\n| **U. Asatkhyātivāda** | 6. The object of error is neither real (*sat*), nor unreal (*asat*), nor both; it is an inexpressible positive entity sublated by right knowledge. |\n\nChoose the correct matching code:",
