      "correct_answer_text": "B) Sāṁkhya argues that since Puruṣa is entirely devoid of the three guṇas (atraiguṇya) and pure witness-consciousness, the experience of being conditioned by diverse guṇic proportions (one person being predominantly sattvic/wise, another rajasic/restless, another tamasic/deluded) requires distinct individual centers of conscious reflection; if there were only one cosmic Puruṣa, the enlightenment or moral striving of one individual would necessitate the identical simultaneous enlightenment of all beings.",
      "grounding": "In Īśvarakṛṣṇa’s *Sāṁkhyakārikā* 18 and Vācaspati Miśra’s *Sāṁkhyatattvakaumudī*:\n- The three arguments for *puruṣa-bahutva* are:\n  1. *Janma-maraṇa-karaṇānāṁ pratiniyamāt*: If there were only one Puruṣa, then when one person is born, everyone would be born; when one dies, all would die; if one is blind, all would be blind. The strict allotment/distribution (*pratiniyama*) of birth, death, and sensory faculties proves distinct selves.\n  2. *Ayugapat-pravṛtteś ca*: Human actions are not synchronized; while Devadatta is meditating, Yajñadatta is sleeping, and Viṣṇumitra is fighting.\n  3. *Traiguṇya-viparyayāc ca*: In the world, we observe beings characterized by radically divergent dominances of the gunas—some are serene and illuminated by *Sattva*, others violent and consumed by *Rajas*, others dull and submerged in *Tamas*. \n- Sāṁkhya’s refutation of the Advaitic monistic counter-thesis:\n  If there were only one single Puruṣa reflected across all mental sheaths (*antaḥkaraṇa*), then the bondage or liberation of that single Puruṣa would be universal. When Śukadeva attained liberation, the entire universe and all living beings would have achieved liberation instantaneously (*sarva-mukti-prasaṅga*). Since one being attains liberation while millions remain bound in suffering, there must be a genuine plurality of distinct Selves (*puruṣa-bahutva*), each standing as an independent witness to its respective empirical evolution of *Prakṛti*.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: Puruṣa is never an active agent (*Puruṣa is akartā*); Puruṣa does not choose or manipulate guṇas.\n- **Option C is incorrect**: Sāṁkhya holds that the plurality of Puruṣas is eternal and absolute, persisting even in *kaivalya*; it never merges into a single entity in *pralaya*.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: In Sāṁkhya, Puruṣa is all-pervading (*vibhu*), not atomic (*aṇu*). The view that the self is atomic belongs to Viśiṣṭādvaita and certain Jain/Vaiṣṇava schools.\n\n---",
