      "question": "Given below are two statements: one is labelled as **Assertion (A)** and the other is labelled as **Reason (R)**:\n\n**Assertion (A)**: In the *Sāṁkhyakārikā* (62), Īśvarakṛṣṇa presents the paradoxical metaphysical conclusion:\n$$\\text{\"tasmān na badhyate 'ddhā na mucyate nāpi saṁsarati kaścit |}$$\n$$\\text{saṁsarati badhyate mucyate ca nānāśrayā prakṛtiḥ ||\"}$$\n(\"Therefore, in truth, no Puruṣa is ever bound, nor is any Puruṣa ever liberated, nor does any Puruṣa transmigrate; it is Prakṛti alone, in her manifold forms, that is bound, transmigrates, and is liberated.\")  \n**Reason (R)**: Because Puruṣa is eternally pure, immutable (*kūṭasthanitya*), unattached (*asaṅga*), and devoid of agency (*akartṛ*), bondage and liberation are phenomenological appearances occurring entirely within the intellect (*buddhi*), which projects its own states onto Puruṣa due to non-discrimination (*aviveka*).\n\nIn light of the above statements, choose the correct option:",
