      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In the *Yogasūtra* of Patañjali (with Vyāsa’s *Yogasūtrabhāṣya* 1.1–1.6), the cessation of the modifications of the mind-field (*citta-vṛtti-nirodha*) is systematically expounded.\n\nEvaluate the following statements regarding the **five Cittabhūmis (planes of mind)** and the **five Cittavṛttis (mental modifications)**:\n\nI. According to Vyāsa, the five *cittabhūmis* are: *Kṣipta* (restless/scattered by Rajas), *Mūḍha* (torpid/infatuated by Tamas), *Vikṣipta* (occasionally steady but distracted), *Ekāgra* (one-pointed), and *Niruddha* (fully restricted).  \nII. Authentic Yoga is possible on all five planes, but *Samprajñāta Samādhi* occurs specifically on the *Vikṣipta* plane, whereas *Asamprajñāta Samādhi* occurs on the *Niruddha* plane.  \nIII. The five *cittavṛttis* are: *Pramāṇa* (valid cognition), *Viparyaya* (erroneous cognition), *Vikalpa* (conceptual construction devoid of an objective referent), *Nidrā* (deep sleep resting on the cognitive presentation of non-existence), and *Smṛti* (memory / retention of experienced objects).  \nIV. *Vikalpa* is defined as *śabda-jñānānupātī vastu-śūnyo vikalpaḥ* (YS 1.9), exemplified by expressions like \"the consciousness of the Puruṣa\" or \"the horns of a hare\", where words generate cognition despite the complete absence of a corresponding ontological entity.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
