      "grounding": "In Patañjali’s *Yogasūtra* and Vyāsa’s *Bhāṣya* (1.1–1.11):\n- **Statement I is correct**: Vyāsa opens the *Yogasūtrabhāṣya* (1.1) by listing the five levels of the mind: *kṣiptaṁ, mūḍhaṁ, vikṣiptaṁ, ekāgraṁ, niruddham iti citta-bhūmayaḥ*.\n- **Statement II is INCORRECT**: Vyāsa explicitly denies that authentic Yoga occurs on the first three levels!\n  - In *Kṣipta* and *Mūḍha*, there is no question of Yoga.\n  - In *Vikṣipta* (the distracted mind that occasionally attains brief focus), the concentration is unstable and instantly washed away by distractions; Vyāsa states: *tatra vikṣipte cetasi vartamāno yogo na yoga-pakṣe vartate* (\"Yoga occurring in the distracted mind does not count as authentic Yoga\").\n  - Authentic *Samprajñāta Samādhi* occurs **only** on the fourth plane (*Ekāgra*), and *Asamprajñāta Samādhi* occurs **only** on the fifth plane (*Niruddha*).\n- **Statement III is correct**: YS 1.6: *pramāṇa-viparyaya-vikalpa-nidrā-smṛtayaḥ*. Nidrā is explicitly defined in YS 1.10 as a positive mental modification resting on the feeling/cognition of absence (*abhāva-pratyayālambanā vṛttir nidrā*), which is why upon waking one remembers: \"I slept peacefully, I knew nothing.\"\n- **Statement IV is correct**: YS 1.9: *śabdajñānānupātī vastuśūnyo vikalpaḥ*. Conceptual construction is cognition sparked by mere verbal expression without any corresponding factual substance (*vastu-śūnya*). When one says \"the consciousness of Puruṣa\" (*caitanyaṁ puruṣasya*), grammar treats Puruṣa and consciousness as possessor and possessed, whereas in reality they are identically one.",
