      "grounding": "In Patañjali’s *Yogasūtra* (1.24–26) and Vyāsa’s *Bhāṣya*:\n- YS 1.24:\n  $$\\text{\"kleśa-karma-vipākāśayair aparāmṛṣṭaḥ puruṣa-viśeṣa īśvaraḥ.\"}$$\n  (\"Īśvara is a distinct Puruṣa, untouched by afflictions, actions, their maturational fruits, or residual subconscious latencies.\")\n- YS 1.25–26:\n  $$\\text{\"tatra niratiśayaṁ sarvajñatva-bījam | sa pūrveṣām api guruḥ kālenānavacchedāt ||\"}$$\n  (\"In Him, the seed of omniscience attains its unexcelled limit. He is the Teacher even of the most ancient seers, being unconditioned by time.\")\n- **Systemic Demarcation**:\n  1. Unlike the **Naiyāyika God**, who is the efficient cause (*nimitta-kāraṇa*) responsible for the physical creation and dissolution of the universe (combining atoms, assigning karma), Patañjali’s Īśvara has **no cosmological role**. He does not create the world; *Prakṛti* evolves spontaneously through her own internal teleology.\n  2. Unlike **Advaita’s Īśvara**, who is Brahman conditioned by cosmic *Māyā*, Patañjali’s Īśvara is an authentic, eternal, individual *Puruṣa* who has never known bondage (*sadā-mukta*).\n  3. Devotion to Him (*īśvara-praṇidhāna*) is introduced primarily as an optional, highly efficacious spiritual technique (*upāya*) to remove mental obstacles (*antarāya*) and achieve *samādhi*.",
