      "correct_answer_text": "A) Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).",
      "grounding": "In Jaimini’s *Mīmāṁsāsūtra* (1.1.2 & 1.1.4: *satsamprayoge puruṣasyendriyāṇāṁ buddhijanma tat pratyakṣam animittaṁ vidyamānopalambhanatvāt*) and Śabarasvāmin’s *Bhāṣya*:\n- Śabara explains why ordinary *pramāṇas* are impotent regarding Dharma:\n  1. *Pratyakṣa* (perception) functions only when an already existing physical object is in direct contact with a functioning sense-organ (*satsamprayoge*). It is restricted to the present moment (*vidyamāna-upalambhana*).\n  2. *Dharma* is not a present physical object; it is an unseen, future-yielding transcendental value (*bhavya-artha*). When an altar is built and ghee is offered, the eye sees only fire and smoke, not Dharma.\n  3. *Anumāna* (inference) depends entirely upon prior perceptual observation of *vyāpti*. Since no human being has ever perceived the relation between the *Agnihotra* sacrifice and attainment of heaven (*svarga*), no inferential mark (*liṅga*) exists to deduce Dharma.\n  4. Therefore, the Vedic injunction (*codanā*—the imperative directive: *yageta*, *kuryāt*) is the sole, sui generis epistemic instrument (*pramāṇa*) capable of commanding and revealing what ought to be done to achieve transcendental wellbeing (*artha*). Hence, (R) provides the exact, profound epistemic and ontological justification for (A).",
