      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In Pūrva Mīmāṁsā hermeneutics (as codified in Āpadeva’s *Mīmāṁsā-Nyāya-Prakāśa* and Laugākṣi Bhāskara’s *Arthasaṁgraha*), Vedic injunctions (*vidhi*) are systematically categorized.\n\nEvaluate the following statements regarding the classification and functions of *Vidhi*:\n\nI. The four-fold functional classification of *vidhi* comprises: *Utpatti-vidhi* (originative injunction), *Viniyoga-vidhi* (injunction of application/auxiliary relations), *Prayoga-vidhi* (injunction of sequential performance), and *Adhikāra-vidhi* (injunction of qualification/ownership of fruits).  \nII. An *Apūrva-vidhi* is an injunction that enjoins an action that is entirely unknown through any other empirical or rational source (e.g., *\"One desiring heaven should sacrifice with the Darśapūrṇamāsa\"*).  \nIII. A *Niyama-vidhi* (restrictive injunction) applies when an action could be accomplished by two alternative empirical methods, and the scripture restricts the agent exclusively to one method (e.g., *\"One threshes the paddy\"*, which restricts the husking of grain to threshing rather than peeling with fingernails).  \nIV. A *Parisaṅkhyā-vidhi* (exclusive injunction) enjoins an act that is simultaneously obtained for two objects, functioning specifically to exclude or prohibit one of them (e.g., *\"Five five-nailed animals may be eaten\"*).\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
