      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, III, and IV",
      "grounding": "In Laugākṣi Bhāskara’s *Arthasaṁgraha* and Āpadeva’s *Mīmāṁsā-Nyāya-Prakāśa*:\n- **Statement I is correct**: *Vidhis* are four-fold by function:\n  1. *Utpatti-vidhi*: Enjoins the bare nature of the rite (e.g., *agnihotraṁ juhoti*).\n  2. *Viniyoga-vidhi*: Determines auxiliary relations (*aṅga-aṅgī-bhāva*, e.g., *dadhā juhoti*).\n  3. *Prayoga-vidhi*: Enjoins the rapid, uninterrupted chronological order of performance.\n  4. *Adhikāra-vidhi*: Specifies who is entitled to reap the fruit of the sacrifice (e.g., *svargakāmo yajeta*).\n- **Statement II is correct**: *Vidhir atyantam aprāptau*—an *Apūrva-vidhi* establishes a command whose subject matter is totally unobtained (*atyantam aprāpta*) through any worldly means.\n- **Statement III is correct**: *Pakṣe prāptau tu niyamaḥ*—a *Niyama-vidhi* operates when an action is alternately obtained (*pakṣe aprāpta*). Paddy can be husked either by threshing with a pestle or by using nails. The injunction *vrīhīn avahanti* (\"he threshes the rice\") restricts the agent to threshing, ruling out nail-peeling for the sake of unseen merit (*adṛṣṭa*).\n- **Statement IV is correct**: *Tatra cānyatra ca prāptāv itara-vyāvṛttiḥ parisaṅkhyā*—a *Parisaṅkhyā-vidhi* applies when an action is simultaneously obtained for two cases, and its purpose is to exclude one case (e.g., *pañca pañcanakhā bhakṣyāḥ* does not command eating five-nailed animals, but excludes the eating of all other five-nailed animals).",
