      "grounding": "In Kumārila Bhaṭṭa’s *Tantravārttika* (on MS 2.1.5) and Śālikanātha Miśra’s *Prakaraṇapañcikā* (*Kāryapārāyaṇa*):\n- **Kumārila’s View**: The momentary physical act of offering oblations perishes in seconds. An extinct act cannot directly produce an effect across centuries. Therefore, the sacrificial action generates an unseen causal potency (*śakti*), termed *Apūrva* (that which was \"not prior\"), which inheres directly in the eternal Self (*Ātman*) of the sacrificer. This *ātma-niṣṭha-apūrva* matures at the end of physical life to produce the heavenly experience.\n- **Prabhākara’s View (Niyogavāda)**: Prabhākara objects that action is insentient and cannot directly impart a quality to the immaterial Self. \n  - Instead, the Vedic sentence centres upon the verbal suffix (*liṅ* / *loṭ* / *tavya*).\n  - The verbal suffix primarily communicates **Kārya** (that which is to be done) or **Niyoga** (the Imperative).\n  - This *Kārya* is sui generis; it is non-empirical, unseen by senses, and therefore termed *Apūrva*.\n  - For Prabhākara, *Apūrva* is not a psychological or substantial trace stamped on the Soul; it is the ontological realization of the Vedic Command (*Niyoga*) which, once prompted by the agent’s commitment, actively persists as an objective moral task until it drives the universe to bestow the appropriate fruit (*kārya-siddhi*).",
