      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In the opening preamble to his commentary on the *Brahmasūtra* (the celebrated *Adhyāsabhāṣya*), Śaṅkarācārya addresses the primary epistemological-metaphysical problem: How can superimposition (*adhyāsa*) occur between the Subject (*viṣayin*, pure consciousness / the 'I') and the Object (*viṣaya*, the insentient realm / the 'non-I'), given that they are as diametrically opposed as light and darkness (*tamaḥ-prakāśavad viruddha-svabhāva*)?\n\nWhat is Śaṅkara’s exact technical definition of *Adhyāsa*, and how does he account for its possibility when the true Self (*Ātman*) is non-sensory and not an object (*aviṣaya*) presented before the eyes?",
      "options": {
        "A": "*Adhyāsa* is defined as *smṛtirūpaḥ paratra pūrvadṛṣṭāvabhāsaḥ* (\"the appearance, in the form of memory, of something previously experienced in another locus\"); it is possible because the *Ātman* is not entirely a non-object (*atyantam aviṣaya*), since it shines self-luminously as the object/referent of the 'I'-cognition (*asmat-pratyaya-viṣayatvāt*) and is immediately self-evident (*aparokṣatvāc ca*).",
        "B": "*Adhyāsa* is defined as the physical transformation of the brain cells under the influence of Rajas and Tamas, proved by dreamless sleep.",
        "C": "*Adhyāsa* is defined as *yathārtha-jñānam*, meaning all superimposition is actually true because God creates illusory objects to test human faith.",
        "D": "*Adhyāsa* is the psychological projection of subconscious desires (*vāsanā*) onto the external world, possible because the soul has an atomic size and travels through the optic nerve.\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) Adhyāsa is defined as smṛtirūpaḥ paratra pūrvadṛṣṭāvabhāsaḥ (\"the appearance, in the form of memory, of something previously experienced in another locus\"); it is possible because the Ātman is not entirely a non-object (atyantam aviṣaya), since it shines self-luminously as the object/referent of the 'I'-cognition (asmat-pratyaya-viṣayatvāt) and is immediately self-evident (aparokṣatvāc ca).",
      "grounding": "In Śaṅkarācārya’s *Adhyāsabhāṣya* (preamble to *Brahmasūtrabhāṣya*):\n- Śaṅkara defines superimposition with technical perfection:\n  $$\\text{\"āha—ko 'yam adhyāso nāmeti? ucyate—smṛti-rūpaḥ paratra pūrva-dṛṣṭāvabhāsaḥ.\"}$$\n  (\"It is asked: What is this superimposition? It is replied: It is the apparent presentation, in the mode of memory, of an object previously perceived in another locus.\")\n- The Purvapakṣa raises a serious objection: Superimposition normally occurs when an object present before the senses (like a piece of nacre) is mistaken for another previously perceived object (silver). But the inner Self (*Pratyagātman*) is partless, non-physical, and ungraspable by the senses (*aviṣaya*). How can the non-self (body, senses, mind) be superimposed upon the Self, or the Self upon the non-self?\n- Śaṅkara resolves this objection definitively:\n  $$\\text{\"na tāvad ayam ekāntenāviṣayaḥ, asmat-pratyaya-viṣayatvād aparokṣatvāc ca pratyag-ātma-prasiddheḥ.\"}$$\n  (\"The Self is not entirely a non-object; for it is known to exist as the object/referent of the 'I'-notion [*asmat-pratyaya*], and because the inner Self is immediately self-evident [*aparokṣa*] to everyone.\")\n  Nobody ever doubts \"Do I exist or do I not exist?\" Consciousness is immediately revealed (*svaprakāśa*). Hence, it possesses sufficient cognitive presence to serve as the substrate (*adhiṣṭhāna*) upon which the psycho-physical apparatus (the 'mine' and the 'I-maker') is mistakenly superimposed through beginningless ignorance (*anādir ananto naisargiko 'dhyāsaḥ*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option B is incorrect**: Advaita does not reduce *adhyāsa* to neuro-physiological or purely bodily transformations; *adhyāsa* is a transcendental, epistemological-metaphysical confusion of the Real and the Unreal (*satya-anṛte mithunī-kṛtya*).\n- **Option C is incorrect**: This is the *Satkhyāti* position of Rāmānuja, which completely rejects Śaṅkara’s doctrine of *adhyāsa* as *mithyā*.\n- **Option D is incorrect**: In Advaita, the *Ātman* is all-pervading (*vibhu*), never atomic (*aṇu*), and *adhyāsa* is not an optic journey through cranial nerves.\n\n---",
