      "title": "Multi-Statement MSQ - The Post-Śaṅkara Schism: Bhāmatī vs. Vivaraṇa)",
      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "Following Śaṅkarācārya, classical Advaita Vedānta split into two prominent scholastic traditions: the **Bhāmatī school** (founded by Vācaspati Miśra) and the **Vivaraṇa school** (founded by Padmapāda and formalized by Prakāśātman).\n\nEvaluate the following statements regarding the doctrinal divergences between these two schools:\n\nI. Regarding the locus (*āśraya*) of cosmic ignorance (*avidyā*), the Bhāmatī school holds that the individual self (*jīva*) is the locus while Brahman is the content/object (*viṣaya*), whereas the Vivaraṇa school insists that Brahman alone is both the locus (*āśraya*) and the object (*viṣaya*) of *avidyā*.  \nII. Regarding the ontological relation between *Jīva* and *Brahman*, the Bhāmatī school upholds the Limitation Theory (**Avacchedavāda**), whereas the Vivaraṇa school upholds the Reflection Theory (**Pratibimbavāda**).  \nIII. Regarding the instrumental cause (*karaṇa*) of liberating direct realization (*aparokṣa-jñāna*), the Bhāmatī school maintains that the mind (*manas*), purified by continuous meditation (*nididhyāsana*), is the direct instrument, whereas the Vivaraṇa school maintains that the scriptural Great Sentences (*Mahāvākyas*) themselves generate immediate perceptual realization (*śabda-aparokṣavāda*).  \nIV. The Bhāmatī school posits a single, cosmic *avidyā* (*ekāvidyāvāda*), whereas the Vivaraṇa school posits an infinite multiplicity of distinct *avidyās* (*nānāvidyāvāda*) to explain why one jīva can be liberated without liberating all others.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I, II, and III only",
        "B": "I and IV only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, and III only",
      "grounding": "In Vācaspati Miśra’s *Bhāmatī* and Prakāśātman’s *Pañcapādikā-Vivarana* (as well as Appayya Dīkṣita’s *Siddhāntaleśasaṁgraha*):\n- **Statement I is correct**: \n  - *Bhāmatī*: The *jīva* is the locus (*āśraya*) of ignorance: *jīvāśrayā viṣayīkṛta-brahma-viṣayā avidyā*. To the objection of mutual dependence (*anyonyāśraya*: jīva depends on avidyā, avidyā depends on jīva), Vācaspati replies that the cycle is beginningless (*bījāṅkuravat anādi*).\n  - *Vivaraṇa*: Brahman is pure non-dual consciousness; there is no jīva prior to avidyā. Hence, Brahman alone is both the substrate (*āśraya*) and the object (*viṣaya*) of avidyā (*āśrayatva-viṣayatva-bhāgī hy eka eva cetanaḥ*).\n- **Statement II is correct**: \n  - *Bhāmatī* defends **Avacchedavāda**: Just as space (*ākāśa*) appears partitioned into pot-space (*ghaṭākāśa*) by the limiting adjunct of the pot, so the partless Brahman appears limited into *jīvas* by the internal organ (*antaḥkaraṇa*).\n  - *Vivaraṇa* defends **Pratibimbavāda**: Brahman is like the original face (*bimba*), and the jīvas are real reflections (*pratibimba*) cast upon the mirror of *avidyā* / *antaḥkaraṇa*. (A variant, *Ābhāsavāda* by Sureśvara, treats the reflection as illusory).\n- **Statement III is correct**: \n  - *Bhāmatī*: Verbal testimony (*śabda*) can only produce mediate knowledge (*parokṣa-jñāna*). Direct experience (*aparokṣa*) requires a sensory faculty; hence, the mind (*manas*), concentrated through *nididhyāsana*, is the true *karaṇa* of liberation.\n  - *Vivaraṇa*: Words can directly produce immediate perception when the object is already present and self-luminous, as in the famous parable: \"You are the tenth man\" (*daśamas tvam asi*). The Upanisadic *Mahāvākya* (*Tat Tvam Asi*) directly produces immediate intuition (*aparokṣa-jñāna*).\n- **Statement IV is INCORRECT**: Statement IV reverses the positions! The **Bhāmatī** school is famous for admitting a **multiplicity of avidyās** (*nānāvidyā-vāda* / *prati-jīvaṁ bhinnā 'vidyā*) to prevent the flaw that when one person is liberated, all would be liberated. Conversely, the **Vivaraṇa** school champions the doctrine of a **single primal ignorance** (**Mūlāvidyā** / *ekāvidyā-vāda*) accompanied by modal states of ignorance (*tūlāvidyā*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since Statement IV inverts the cardinal positions on *ekāvidyā* vs. *nānāvidyā*, any option containing IV (B, C, and D) is definitively eliminated.\n\n---",
