      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B) If Avidyā conceals Brahman, this concealment (tirodhāna) must mean either the destruction of the luminous nature of Brahman (prakāśa-nāśa) or the obstruction of the production of light (prakāśotpāda-pratibandha). But since Brahman's nature is eternally unoriginated and self-luminous pure consciousness (svaprakāśa-svarūpa), to say that His luminosity is concealed means that His very nature is destroyed, which is an absurdity.",
      "grounding": "In Rāmānuja’s *Śrībhāṣya* (1.1.1, *Mahāsiddhānta*):\n- Rāmānuja formulates the second *anupapatti*—*Tirodhānānupapatti*:\n  $$\\text{\"prakāśa-tirodhānaṁ nāma prakāśa-nāśa eva |}$$\n  $$\\text{prakāśasya svarūpatve svarūpa-nāśa eva syāt ||\"}$$\n- The dilemma is devastating:\n  1. The Advaitin claims that cosmic *Avidyā* conceals the true nature of Brahman (*Brahma-svarūpāvaraṇa*), causing the world-appearance.\n  2. Rāmānuja asks: What is the meaning of \"concealment of light\" (*prakāśa-tirodhāna*)?\n  3. In ordinary life, if light is produced by a lamp, concealment means obstructing the generation of light or obstructing the visual rays.\n  4. But in Advaita, Brahman does not *have* consciousness or produce light; Brahman *is* pure consciousness itself (*prakāśa eva svarūpam*).\n  5. If the self-luminosity of Brahman is obstructed or obscured by *Avidyā*, that means the light does not shine. But if the light does not shine, then since the light is Brahman’s very essence, Brahman ceases to exist!\n  6. To say that eternal, immutable, self-shining consciousness is \"covered\" while remaining existent is a contradiction in terms. Therefore, *tirodhāna* (concealment) of a purely self-luminous monad by an external principle of darkness is an ontological impossibility.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect**: In Advaita, Brahman is Nirguna; Rāmānuja does not argue from physical \"ornaments\", but from the foundational Advaitic definition of Brahman as *Prakāśa-svarūpa*.\n- **Options C and D are incorrect**: These are frivolous trivializations that completely miss Rāmānuja’s technical metaphysical dilemma on *prakāśa-nāśa*.\n\n---",
