      "grounding": "In Rāmānuja’s *Śrībhāṣya* (on *Brahmasūtra* 2.1.9) and *Vedārthasaṁgraha*:\n- Rāmānuja rejects both the Advaitic view (that the world is an unreal appearance, *vivarta*) and the Dvaita view (that souls/matter are completely disconnected, independent substances externally related to God).\n- To preserve the absolute non-duality of the organic whole (*Viśiṣṭa-advaita*—non-duality of the qualified whole) while maintaining the reality of the distinct parts, Rāmānuja introduces **Apṛthaksiddhi** (inseparable relation).\n- How is this relation formulated? As **Śarīra-Śarīrī-bhāva** (the relation of the body to the embodied soul).\n- Rāmānuja supplies the rigorous philosophical definition of *śarīra* (*Śrībhāṣya* 2.1.9):\n  $$\\text{\"yasya cetanasya yad dravyaṁ sarvātmanā svārthe niyantum dhārayituṁ ca śakyam,}$$\n  $$\\text{tac-cheṣatai-ka-svarūpaṁ ca tat tasya śarīram iti śarīra-lakṣaṇam.\"}$$\n  (\"Any substance which a conscious entity can completely control and support for its own purposes, and whose sole essential nature is to be an auxiliary/subservient part [*śeṣa*] to that entity, is the body of that conscious entity.\")\n- Since all conscious souls (*cit*) and all material nature (*acit*) are supported (*dhārya*), controlled (*niyāmya*), and exist solely to serve the glory (*śeṣin*) of the Supreme Lord (*Parabrahman* / Nārāyaṇa), the cosmos constitutes His living body. Hence, (R) provides the exact, profound theological-metaphysical demonstration of (A).",
