      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In the *Tattvaviveka* and *Anuvyākhyāna*, Madhvācārya establishes the philosophy of **Dvaita (Bhedavāda)**, asserting that difference (*bheda*) is the foundational, irreducible feature of reality.\n\nEvaluate the following statements regarding Madhva’s metaphysical taxonomy:\n\nI. The **Pañcabheda** (Five Great Differences) are absolute, eternal, and real: difference between God and Jīva, between God and Matter (*Jaḍa*), between Jīva and Matter, between individual Jīvas, and between individual material objects.  \nII. Madhva classifies reality into two ultimate ontological orders: **Svatantra** (Independent), which is Lord Viṣṇu alone; and **Paratantra** (Dependent), which encompasses all finite souls and insentient nature, whose existence, knowledge, and activity are eternally subordinate to Viṣṇu.  \nIII. To explain how an entity can be non-different from its qualities while still permitting conceptual distinctions (e.g., how Viṣṇu is non-different from His bliss, yet we speak of \"Viṣṇu's bliss\"), Madhva introduces the unique ontological category of **Viśeṣa** (*abhinna-viśeṣa*).  \nIV. Madhva rejects the doctrine of *Tāratamya* (spiritual gradation among souls), maintaining that all liberated souls experience identical, uniform bliss in Vaikuṇṭha.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I, II, and III only",
        "B": "I and IV only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV\n\n---"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A) I, II, and III only",
      "grounding": "In Madhvācārya’s *Tattvaviveka* (verses 1–3) and Jayatīrtha’s *Nyāyasudhā*:\n- **Statement I is correct**: *Tattvaviveka* 1 opens with:\n  $$\\text{\"jīveśvarabhidā caiva jaḍeśvarabhidā tathā |}$$\n  $$\\text{jīva-bhedo mithaś caiva jaḍa-jīva-bhidā tathā |}$$\n  $$\\text{mithaś ca jaḍa-bhedo 'yam prapañco bheda-pañcakaḥ ||\"}$$\n  These five differences constitute the eternal, mind-independent structure of the cosmos.\n- **Statement II is correct**: Madhva bifurcates reality into *Svatantra* (the independently real: Lord Nārāyaṇa/Viṣṇu alone) and *Paratantra* (the dependently real: *Cit* and *Acit*). The dependent entities are completely real, but they derive their being (*sattā*), cognition (*pramiti*), and activity (*pravṛtti*) exclusively from the will of the Independent Sovereign.\n- **Statement III is correct**: How can we say \"the cloth's whiteness\" or \"Brahman's knowledge\" if substance and attribute are identical? The Naiyāyika posits *samavāya* (a distinct relation), which leads to regress. Madhva posits **Viśeṣa**: the inherent capacity in an undivided entity to function as if there were a distinction of relata, without introducing an actual ontological cleavage (*bhedābhāve 'pi bheda-kārya-kāri*).\n- **Statement IV is INCORRECT**: Statement IV directly contradicts one of Madhva’s most famous and radical doctrines: **Svarūpa-tāratamya** (the innate hierarchy of souls). Madhva categorizes souls into three eternal classes: *Mukti-yogya* (destined for liberation), *Nitya-saṁsārin* (eternally revolving in transmigration), and *Tamo-yogya* (destined for eternal damnation/Andhatamas). Even among the liberated souls in Vaikuṇṭha, there is an eternal hierarchy (*tāratamya*) of bliss based on their innate spiritual capacity (*svarūpa-ānanda*); the bliss of Brahmā is greater than that of Indra, which is greater than that of ordinary liberated souls.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since Statement IV asserts that Madhva rejects *Tāratamya* and accepts uniform bliss (which is the exact opposite of Madhva's doctrine), any option containing IV (B, C, and D) is invalid.\n\n---",
