      "correct_answer_text": "B) While Śaṅkara's Advaita defines ultimate reality as static, featureless consciousness (Śuddha-Prakāśa / Nirviśeṣa-Caitanya) and relegates dynamic creativity and world-manifestation to an inexplicable principle of illusion (Māyā / Anirvacanīya), Kashmir Śaivism asserts that consciousness is intrinsically dynamic: it is both self-luminous light (Prakāśa) and sovereign self-reflective active consciousness (Vimarśa / Svātantrya); the world is not an illusion, but the real, joyous self-expansion (sphurattā / unmeṣa) of Paramasiva appearing on the mirror of His own divine being.",
      "grounding": "In Utpaladeva’s *Īśvarapratyabhijñākārikā* (1.5.11–14) and Abhinavagupta’s *Vimarśinī*:\n- Utpaladeva critiques the Advaitin conception of Brahman as mere static illumination:\n  $$\\text{\"svāminas ca ātma-saṁsthasya viśvasyātmatayā nayan |}$$\n  $$\\text{prakāśaḥ syāt tad-arthaṁ tu vimarśaḥ syān na kevalaḥ ||\"}$$\n  If consciousness were mere *Prakāśa* (bare luminosity without self-reflection), it would be insentient and inert, like a crystal or a mirror reflecting objects passively (*jaḍaprāyo vimarśa-vihīnaḥ prakāśaḥ*).\n- What makes consciousness sentient and divine is **Vimarśa**: the sovereign, autonomous power of self-awareness (*ahaṁ-parāmarśa*), creative free will (*svātantrya*), and pulsating energy (*spanda* / *sphurattā*).\n- Under **Svātantryavāda** (*Ābhāsavāda*):\n  1. The universe is not an illusory projection (*vivarta*) born of cosmic defect or ignorance (*avidyā*).\n  2. The universe is the free, glorious self-manifestation (*unmeṣa*) of Paramasiva. Just as a yogi or a king manifests cities within his imagination through his mere will, Paramasiva projects the 36 tattvas of the cosmos upon Himself as the screen (*bhitti*), through His own inherent sovereignty (*svātantrya-śakti*).\n  3. The world is entirely real and divine because it is the embodiment of Śiva Himself (*sarvaṁ śivamayam*).",
