      "grounding": "In Bhāskarācārya’s *Brahmasūtrabhāṣya* and Nimbārkācārya’s *Vedāntapārijātasaurabha* (with Śrīnivāsa’s *Vedāntakaustubha*):\n- **Bhāskara’s Aupādhika-Bhedābheda**:\n  - Non-difference (*abheda*) is the innate, essential, and eternal nature of Brahman (*svābhāvika*).\n  - Difference (*bheda*) is temporary and adventitious (*aupādhika*), generated by the physical and mental adjuncts (*upādhis*). When the adjuncts dissolve in liberation (*mukti*), the difference disappears completely, and the soul merges into non-differentiated identity with Brahman.\n- **Nimbārka’s Svābhāvika-Bhedābheda**:\n  - Nimbārka rejects Bhāskara's asymmetry. He argues that if difference were merely *aupādhika* (due to adjuncts), it would either be unreal (leading back to Śaṅkara's *Māyā*) or an unexplained defect corrupting Brahman.\n  - Therefore, Nimbārka maintains that **both difference and non-difference are equally natural and eternal (svābhāvika)**.\n  - The relation is analogized to the sun and its rays (*sūrya-kiraṇa*) or the sea and its waves (*taraṅga*). The rays are non-different from the sun because they cannot exist independently of it; yet they are different from the sun because an individual ray is not the entire solar orb.\n  - The individual soul (*jīva*) and matter (*acit*) are different from Brahman because they have distinct dependent natures; yet they are non-different because their very existence and functioning are inseparably sustained by Brahman. This dual status of *bheda* and *abheda* endures even in *mokṣa*.",
