      "title": "Leibniz's *Monadology*: Simple Substances, Windows, and Pre-Established Harmony",
      "format_type": "Multi-Statement Evaluation / MSQ (Format C)",
      "question": "**Format**: Multi-Statement Evaluation / MSQ (Format C)\n\nIn the *Monadology* (1714) and *New System of Nature* (1695), G.W. Leibniz formulates his dynamic, pluralistic metaphysics. Consider the following statements:\n\n1. Monads are simple substances (*substances simples*) that have no parts, no extension, and no figure; they are the true atoms of nature and the elements of things.\n2. Monads have \"no windows through which anything could come in or go out\" (*les monades n'ont point de fenêtres*); no created substance can exert real physical or influxive causal power on the interior of another monad.\n3. Every monad is an active, living mirror of the entire universe (*un miroir vivant perpétuel de l'univers*), expressing all other monads from its own unique point of view according to internal principles of perception (*perception*) and appetition (*appétition*).\n4. Leibniz distinguishes sharply between bare **Perception** (the internal representation of external multiplicity) and **Apperception** (the reflective consciousness of this inner state), arguing that the Cartesians made a grave error by ignoring unconscious perceptions (*petites perceptions*) and equating perception solely with apperception.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly correct?",
      "options": {
        "A": "1, 2, and 3 only",
        "B": "2, 3, and 4 only",
        "C": "1, 3, and 4 only",
        "D": "1, 2, 3, and 4"
      },
      "correct_option": "D",
      "correct_answer_text": "(D)",
      "grounding": "- *Statement 1 is correct*: *Monadology* §1-3: The monad is a simple substance without parts. Where there are no parts, there can be neither extension, nor form, nor divisibility. Monads are metaphysical points or \"formal atoms.\"\n- *Statement 2 is correct*: *Monadology* §7: *\"Monads have no windows through which anything could enter or depart.\"* Leibniz decisively rejects the scholastic doctrine of \"physical influx\" (*influxus physicus*), wherein accidents travel from one substance to another. Every monad's changes unfold purely from its own internal principle.\n- *Statement 3 is correct*: *Monadology* §56-57: Due to the interconnectedness of all things, each monad has relations that express all the others, serving as a perpetual living mirror of the universe.\n- *Statement 4 is correct*: *Monadology* §14: Leibniz chastises the Cartesians:\n  > *\"The Cartesians made a great mistake, having considered perceptions of which we are not conscious as nothing. This is also what led them to believe that only minds were monads, and that there are no animal souls.\"*\n  Leibniz shows that we have countless *petites perceptions* (minute, insensible perceptions, like hearing the roar of the ocean composed of thousands of individual imperceptible wave sounds) without *apperception* (reflective conscious awareness).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Since all four statements accurately represent Leibniz's core metaphysical tenets in the *Monadology* (§1-14, 56-57), option (D) is the only logically correct selection.",
      "reference": "G.W. Leibniz, *Monadology*, sections 1-14, 56-62; *New System of Nature*; Nicholas Rescher, *The Monadology of Leibniz*, University of Pittsburgh Press.\n\n---"
