      "title": "Doctrinal Matching: Logic and Language in Early Analytic Philosophy",
      "format_type": "Comparative / Doctrinal Matching (Advanced)",
      "question": "**Format**: Comparative / Doctrinal Matching (Advanced)  \n**Topic**: Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein (*Tractatus*), and Carnap\n\nMatch the distinctive philosophical theses/notions in **List-I** with their exact theoretical formulation or exponent in **List-II**:\n\n| List-I (Philosophical Thesis / Principle) | List-II (Theoretical Formulation / Exponent) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **p. Context Principle (*Kontextprinzip*)** | **1. The fundamental thought (*Grundgedanke*) that logical constants do not represent; logic mirrors the formal scaffolding of the world without asserting anything.** |\n| **q. Incomplete Symbols (*symboles incomplets*)** | **2. Expressions that have no meaning in isolation, but only in the context of an asserted sentence, eliminating ontological commitment to non-existent grammatical subjects.** |\n| **r. The Fundamental Thought of the *Tractatus*** | **3. \"Never ask for the meaning of a word in isolation, but only in the context of a proposition\" (*Grundlagen der Arithmetik*).** |\n| **s. The Principle of Tolerance (*Toleranzprinzip*)** | **4. \"It is not our business to set up prohibitions, but to arrive at conventions... in logic there are no morals. Everyone is at liberty to build up his own logic, i.e., his own form of language.\"** |\n\nChoose the correct matching code:",
      "options": {
        "A": "p-3, q-2, r-1, s-4",
        "B": "p-2, q-3, r-4, s-1",
        "C": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4",
        "D": "p-4, q-2, r-1, s-3"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A",
      "grounding": "- **p matches 3**: Gottlob Frege introduced the **Context Principle** in the Introduction to *Die Grundlagen der Arithmetik* (1884): \"Nach der Bedeutung der Wörter muss im Satzzusammenhange, nicht in ihrer Vereinzelung gefragt werden.\" It serves to block psychologism: we are not led to imagine mental images when considering numbers, because numbers have meaning only as autonomous objects designated in the context of whole numerical propositions.\n- **q matches 2**: Bertrand Russell introduced the doctrine of **incomplete symbols** in \"On Denoting\" (1905) and *Principia Mathematica* (1910). Definite descriptions and class terms do not have a referent of their own in isolation; their contribution to meaning is fully unpacked only when the contextual definition of the entire proposition is unfolded into existential quantification.\n- **r matches 1**: In the *Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus* (proposition 4.0312), Ludwig Wittgenstein explicitly declares what he considers his own most vital insight: \"My fundamental thought (*Mein Grundgedanke*) is that the 'logical constants' do not represent. That the logic of the facts cannot be represented.\" This directly refutes Frege and Russell, both of whom treated logical connectives as referring to logical objects or relations.\n- **s matches 4**: Rudolf Carnap formulated the famous **Principle of Tolerance** (*Toleranzprinzip*) in *The Logical Syntax of Language* (*Logische Syntax der Sprache*, 1934, §17): \"In logic, there are no morals. Everyone is at liberty to build up his own logic, i.e., his own form of language, as he wishes. All that is required of him is that, if he wishes to discuss it, he must state his methods clearly, and give syntactical rules instead of philosophical arguments.\"\n\nThus, the exact correspondence is **p-3, q-2, r-1, s-4**.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- Options B, C, and D disrupt the historic and textual alignment:\n  - In B, p is matched to 2 (confusing Frege's Context Principle with Russell's incomplete symbols).\n  - In C, q is matched to 1 and r to 2 (erroneously assigning the *Grundgedanke* to Russell).\n  - In D, p is matched to 4 (confusing Frege's Context Principle with Carnap's Principle of Tolerance).\n\n**Primary Sources**: \n- G. Frege, *Die Grundlagen der Arithmetik* (1884).\n- B. Russell, \"On Denoting\" (*Mind*, 1905).\n- L. Wittgenstein, *Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus* (1921/1922, 4.0312).\n- R. Carnap, *Logische Syntax der Sprache* (1934, §17).",
      "reference": ""
