      "title": "Conceptual Distinction — Camus on the Absurd and Suicide)",
      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In *The Myth of Sisyphus* (*Le Mythe de Sisyphe*, 1942), Albert Camus identifies the **Absurd** as the fundamental metaphysical condition of human consciousness. Why does Camus reject both **physical suicide** and **philosophical suicide**, and what constitutes the authentic response of the Absurd Hero?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Physical suicide is rejected because it violates Catholic natural law; philosophical suicide is rejected because it fails to achieve scientific materialism; the absurd hero enters monastic quietism.",
        "B": "The Absurd does not reside in the human mind alone nor in the physical universe alone, but in their mutual confrontation—the collision between the human longing for clarity and the unreasonable silence of the world. Physical suicide eliminates the absurd by destroying one of the terms (the human mind); philosophical suicide (the existential leap of faith) eliminates the absurd by fabricating a transcendent meaning; the authentic absurd hero must preserve the tension through perpetual **Revolt**, **Freedom**, and **Passion**.",
        "C": "Physical suicide is embraced by Camus as the ultimate act of metaphysical liberty, whereas philosophical suicide is condemned as cowardice; the absurd hero commits physical suicide to prove absolute independence from nature.",
        "D": "The Absurd is a psychological neurosis that can be eradicated through psychoanalytic therapy; Sisyphus rolls the boulder up the mountain because he is trapped in an unconscious repetition compulsion."
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B",
      "grounding": "In *The Myth of Sisyphus* (Chapter 1, \"An Absurd Reasoning\"), Camus opens with his famous proposition: \"There is but one truly serious philosophical problem, and that is suicide.\"\n\n1. **The Nature of the Absurd:** Camus defines the Absurd not as a static property of objects, but as a relation of disproportion: \"The absurd is born of this confrontation between the human need [for unity, reason, purpose] and the unreasonable silence of the world.\"\n2. **Critique of Physical Suicide:** The person who commits physical suicide attempts to solve the problem by annihilating consciousness. But this is an evasion, not a solution: it destroys one term of the contradiction (the human being), thereby dissolving the confrontation. Suicide is a surrender (*l'échappatoire*).\n3. **Critique of Philosophical Suicide (*le suicide philosophique*):** Camus directs this critique against existentialist thinkers (Kierkegaard, Chestov, Jaspers). Confronted with the terrifying wall of the absurd, they make a \"leap\" (*le saut*) into the supernatural, positing God, a transcendent meaning, or an eternal life. Camus calls this *philosophical suicide*: it kills human reason by forcing it to bow down before that which defies reason. It escapes the absurd by inventing an imaginary cosmic purpose.\n4. **The Absurd Hero:** To live authentically is to live without appeal (*vivre sans appel*), keeping the absurd alive in all its raw lucidity. This demands three consequences:\n   - **Revolt** (*la révolte*): Constant, conscious defiance against meaninglessness without capitulation.\n   - **Freedom** (*la liberté*): The unencumbered freedom of the condemned prisoner who has no future promises.\n   - **Passion** (*la passion*): Experiencing the maximum quantity of life in the present.\n   Sisyphus, condemned for eternity to roll a stone to the mountain crest only to watch it roll back down, is the supreme absurd hero: \"His scorn of the gods, his hatred of death, and his passion for life won him that unspeakable penalty... One must imagine Sisyphus happy.\"",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect:** Camus is an atheist who explicitly rejects Christian theological frameworks and monastic withdrawal.\n- **Option C is incorrect:** It directly contradicts the foundational thesis of the entire book: Camus explicitly condemns physical suicide as an act of defeat and escapism.\n- **Option D is incorrect:** It reduces an ontological and existential diagnosis to clinical pathology, completely misunderstanding Camus's philosophical allegory.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Albert Camus, *The Myth of Sisyphus and Other Essays* (1942), trans. Justin O'Brien (Vintage, 1955), \"Absurd Walls,\" \"Philosophical Suicide,\" and \"The Myth of Sisyphus,\" pp. 3–65, 119–123.\n\n---",
      "reference": ""
