      "question": "In *The History of Sexuality, Volume 1: An Introduction* (*La Volonté de savoir*, 1976), Foucault challenges conventional assumptions regarding power and sex. Consider the following propositions:\n\nI. Foucault formulates the **Repressive Hypothesis** precisely to affirm it, demonstrating that the Victorian era successfully silenced, suppressed, and obliterated sexual discourse from modern Western institutions.  \nII. Rather than silencing sex, modern power generated an explosive incitement to discourse (*mise en discours du sexe*), demanding that sex be endlessly spoken about, analyzed, classified, and institutionalized in medicine, psychiatry, and pedagogy.  \nIII. Foucault defines **Biopower** (*biopouvoir*) as having two poles: an **anatomo-politics of the human body** (disciplining the individual body as a machine) and a **biopolitics of the population** (regulating biological processes of the species: birth rates, mortality, longevity, hygiene).  \nIV. Under biopower, the ancient sovereign right to \"take life or let live\" (*faire mourir ou laisser vivre*) is inverted into the modern biopolitical imperative to \"make live or let die\" (*faire vivre ou rejeter dans la mort / laisser mourir*).\n\nWhich of the above statements are **CORRECT**?",
