      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In *Concluding Unscientific Postscript to Philosophical Fragments* (*Afsluttende uvidenskabelig Efterskrift*, 1846), written under the pseudonym Johannes Climacus, Søren Kierkegaard launches an unrelenting assault against G.W.F. Hegel's speculative idealism. Consider the following propositions:\n\nI. Climacus asserts the famous epistemological thesis: **\"Subjectivity is Truth\"** (*Subjektiviteten er Sandheden*), meaning that the highest truth accessible to an existing human being is not an abstract objective result, but an objective uncertainty held fast in an appropriation-process of the most passionate inwardness.  \nII. Climacus argues that an \"objective system of existence\" is impossible for any existing human being, because existence is always in the process of becoming (*Vorden*), whereas a completed philosophical system requires that existence be finished and closed.  \nIII. Climacus praises Hegel for being the first philosopher to successfully live inside his own philosophical system, thereby unifying speculative logic with everyday historical existence.  \nIV. Hegelian speculative philosophy forgets that the philosopher is an **existing individual** (*en existerende Individ*), mistaking the speculative thinker for a disembodied world-spirit contemplating world history *sub specie aeterni*.\n\nWhich of the above statements are **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I and III only",
        "B": "I, II, and IV only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV"
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B",
      "grounding": "- **Statement I is correct:** In *Concluding Unscientific Postscript* (Part Two, Chapter 2), Climacus provides his celebrated definition: \"An objective uncertainty, held fast through appropriation with the most passionate inwardness, is the truth, the highest truth there is for an existing person.\" Truth in the existential sphere is not what is believed (objective propositional content), but *how* it is believed (the subjective passion of inwardness).\n- **Statement II is correct:** In Part Two, Chapter 2 (§1), Climacus formulates his decisive paradox: \"A system of logic is possible; a system of existence is impossible.\" Why? Because a system presupposes finality, completion, and closure. But human existence is dynamic, open, unfinished, and in constant movement (*Vorden*). Only God, outside of time, could possess a system of existence; an existing human who claims to possess such a system is living in comic self-delusion.\n- **Statement III is completely incorrect:** Kierkegaard relentlessly satirizes Hegel on this exact point! Climacus compares Hegel to a man who builds an enormous, magnificent palace (the speculative system), but himself lives in a small, miserable shack or doghouse next door. Hegel’s personal life, anxiety, sickness, and death were never incorporated into his abstract logical categories.\n- **Statement IV is correct:** Throughout the *Postscript*, Climacus attacks speculative thinkers for adopting an illusory God’s-eye perspective (*sub specie aeternitatis*). By imagining that they are the pure \"World-Historical Spirit,\" Hegelians forget that they are mortal, finite, existing human beings who must make concrete ethical and religious choices.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect:** It includes the false Statement III and omits Statements II and IV.\n- **Option C is incorrect:** It includes Statement III.\n- **Option D is incorrect:** It overlooks Climacus's famous satire of Hegel's refusal to live in his own palace.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Søren Kierkegaard (Johannes Climacus), *Concluding Unscientific Postscript to Philosophical Fragments* (1846), trans. Howard V. Hong and Edna H. Hong (Princeton University Press, 1992), Vol. 1, Part Two, Section II, Chapters 1–2, pp. 189–251.\n\n---",
