      "format_type": "D",
      "question": "Match the technical phenomenological concept in **List-I** with its precise philosophical definition and author in **List-II**:\n\n| List-I (Concept) | List-II (Definition & Exponent) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **p. Eidetic Reduction (*Wesensschau*)** | **1.** The ontological horizon of average everydayness in which Dasein interprets itself through public, anonymous conventions, losing its ownmost potentiality-for-Being. (Heidegger) |\n| **q. Intentional Inexistence (*intentionale Inexistenz*)** | **2.** The pre-predicative, intuitive horizon of common human practice and perceptual evidence which grounds the Galilean mathematization of modern physical science. (Husserl) |\n| **r. *Das Man* (The \"They\")** | **3.** The mental property whereby every mental phenomenon contains within itself something as an object, serving as the distinguishing mark between the physical and the psychical. (Brentano) |\n| **s. *Lebenswelt* (The Lifeworld)** | **4.** The systematic process of free imaginative variation (*freie Variation*) through which empirical facts are stripped of contingency to grasp invariant ideal essences. (Husserl) |\n\nChoose the correct matching code:",
      "options": {
        "A": "p-4, q-3, r-1, s-2",
        "B": "p-3, q-4, r-2, s-1",
        "C": "p-4, q-1, r-3, s-2",
        "D": "p-2, q-3, r-1, s-4"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A",
      "grounding": "- **p matches 4 (Husserl):** In *Cartesian Meditations* (§34) and *Phänomenologische Psychologie*, the **Eidetic Reduction** (*Wesensschau*) proceeds via *free imaginative variation* (*freie Variation*). The phenomenologist takes an empirical particular (e.g., this actual red rose or this tone) and arbitrarily varies its features in fantasy. Those features that can be removed without dissolving the object's identity as a \"spatial thing\" or \"melody\" are contingent; that invariant core without which the object cannot be conceived is its *eidos* (essence).\n- **q matches 3 (Brentano):** In *Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint* (*Psychologie vom empirischen Standpunkte*, 1874), Franz Brentano famously formulates the thesis of intentionality: \"Every mental phenomenon is characterized by what the Scholastics of the Middle Ages called the intentional (or mental) inexistence of an object... In perception something is perceived, in thought something is thought, in hate something is hated.\" This constitutes the irreducible *mark of the mental*.\n- **r matches 1 (Heidegger):** In *Being and Time* (§§26–27), *Das Man* is the public, anonymous \"They\" that governs the average everydayness (*Alltäglichkeit*) of Dasein, determining how one laughs, reads, judges, and lives, leveling down authentic possibilities.\n- **s matches 2 (Husserl):** In *The Crisis of European Sciences and Transcendental Phenomenology* (*Die Krisis der europäischen Wissenschaften*, 1936), Husserl uncovers the **Lebenswelt** (Lifeworld) as the forgotten, pre-theoretical, intuitable ground of meaning upon which Galileo and subsequent physicists constructed the mathematical \"garment of ideas\" (*Ideenkleid*).",
      "distractor_analysis": "All other matching codes (B, C, D) scramble the historical authors and technical definitions. For instance, code B pairs Eidetic Reduction with Brentano, which is historically false; code C pairs intentional inexistence with Heidegger's *Das Man*, committing a fundamental category mistake.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Franz Brentano, *Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint* (1874); Edmund Husserl, *Cartesian Meditations* (1931) & *The Crisis of European Sciences* (1936); Martin Heidegger, *Being and Time* (1927).\n\n---",
