      "title": "Multi-Statement Evaluation — Brentano and Early Phenomenological Intentionality)",
      "format_type": "C",
      "question": "In his groundbreaking work *Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint* (1874), Franz Brentano reintroduced the scholastic doctrine of **intentionality** to contemporary philosophy. Consider the following assertions regarding Brentano’s original doctrine and Husserl's subsequent radicalization of it:\n\nI. Brentano defined mental phenomena as uniquely possessing intentional inexistence (*intentionale Inexistenz*), which signifies the directedness of consciousness toward an immanent intentional object (*immanente Gegenständlichkeit*).  \nII. In his earliest formulation, Brentano maintained that every mental act is either a presentation (*Vorstellung*) or founded upon a presentation.  \nIII. Husserl, in the *Logical Investigations* (Fifth Investigation), fully accepted Brentano’s claim that intentional objects reside literally and immanently inside the real psychological consciousness as mental contents.  \nIV. Brentano's later \"reism\" (*Reismus*) insisted that only concrete, individual things exist, leading him to abandon entifying mental states or abstract relations.\n\nWhich of the above statements are **CORRECT**?",
      "options": {
        "A": "I and III only",
        "B": "I, II, and IV only",
        "C": "II, III, and IV only",
        "D": "I, II, III, and IV"
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B",
      "grounding": "- **Statements I and II are correct:** In *Psychologie vom empirischen Standpunkte* (1874, Book II, Chapter 1), Brentano defines mental phenomena through two foundational theses:\n  1. *Intentional Inexistence:* Mental phenomena contain an object within themselves intentionally (*immanente Gegenständlichkeit*), whereas physical phenomena do not.\n  2. *Priority of Presentation:* Brentano classifies mental phenomena into presentations (*Vorstellungen*), judgments (*Urteile*), and phenomena of love and hate / emotions (*Gemütsbewegungen*). He explicitly declares that nothing can be judged or felt without first being presented: every mental act is either a presentation or founded upon one.\n- **Statement III is incorrect:** This captures the monumental rupture between Brentano and Husserl. In the *Fifth Logical Investigation* (§§11–14, \"Consciousness as Intentional Experience\"), Husserl directly attacks Brentano’s concept of \"intentional inexistence\" if understood as meaning that the object *literally exists inside* the mind as a real component (*reeller Bestandteil*). Husserl argues that if I think of the Emperor of Germany or the planet Jupiter, Jupiter is not physically or psychically inside my head! The object is *transcendent*; what is immanent to consciousness is the *act* (the intentional experience with its meaning-bestowing quality), which intends the transcendent object. Confusing the intended object with an immanent mental content was Brentano's fatal step toward psychologism, which Husserl eradicated.\n- **Statement IV is correct:** In his late philosophy (dictations after 1904, published in *The Theory of Categories* and *The True and the Evident*), Brentano adopted **Reism** (*Reismus*), an extreme nominalistic ontology holding that only concrete, individual things (*res*) exist. He explicitly rejected states of affairs (*Sachverhalte*), abstract universals, and non-real entities, asserting that talk of \"intentional relations\" is merely a linguistic manner of speaking about a thinking person.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect:** It includes the false Statement III and omits the correct Statements II and IV.\n- **Option C is incorrect:** It includes Statement III.\n- **Option D is incorrect:** It uncritically marks Statement III as true, failing to recognize Husserl's famous critique of Brentano in *Logical Investigations*.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Franz Brentano, *Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint* (1874), Book II, Ch. 1; Edmund Husserl, *Logical Investigations*, Vol. 2 (1900/1901), Fifth Investigation (\"On Intentional Experiences and Their 'Contents'\"), §§11–14.\n\n---",
      "reference": ""
