      "title": "Conceptual Distinction — Michael Tooley on the Self-Consciousness Requirement)",
      "format_type": "A",
      "question": "In *\"Abortion and Infanticide\"* (1972), Michael Tooley formulates the **Self-Consciousness Requirement** for possessing a serious Right to Life. What is this requirement, and what controversial ethical conclusion does Tooley logically derive from it regarding neonates?",
      "options": {
        "A": "An entity has a right to life if it possesses human DNA; therefore, infanticide is identical to high treason.",
        "B": "An entity can have a serious right to life only if it possesses the concept of a continuing self as a subject of experiences and other mental states, and believes that it is itself such an entity; since human newborn infants (neonates) do not yet possess the concept of a continuing enduring self, they do not possess a serious right to life, rendering infanticide in principle morally permissible.",
        "C": "An entity has a right to life if it can survive independently of atmospheric oxygen; neonates have a right to life because they breathe air.",
        "D": "An entity has a right to life if and only if it has received legal baptism by an ecclesiastical institution."
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B",
      "grounding": "In *\"Abortion and Infanticide\"* (*Philosophy & Public Affairs*, Vol. 2, No. 1, Autumn 1972), Michael Tooley undertakes a rigorous meta-ethical and conceptual analysis of rights:\n\n1. **The Nature of Rights and Desires:** Tooley argues that there is an intrinsic conceptual link between having a **right** and having a **desire**:\n   - Generally, $A$ has a right to $X$ means that if $A$ desires $X$, then others have a *prima facie* obligation not to deprive $A$ of $X$.\n   - Therefore, to have a **Right to Life** means to have a right to continue existing as a subject of experiences.\n   - For an entity to have a right to its continuing existence, it must be capable of *desiring* its continuing existence.\n2. **The Self-Consciousness Requirement:** But one cannot desire something without possessing the concept of that thing!\n   - To desire to continue existing as a subject of experiences, an entity must possess the **concept of a continuing self**—that is, the concept of a subject of mental states that endures through time.\n   - Moreover, the entity must believe that it itself is such an ongoing subject.\n3. **The Radical Consequence for Infanticide:** \n   - A human fetus lacks this psychological architecture completely.\n   - Crucially, **human newborn infants (neonates)** also lack self-consciousness: they have sensations of hunger or warmth, but do not possess the conceptual apparatus of a \"continuing enduring self through time.\"\n   - Consequently, Tooley arrives at the logically rigorous, highly controversial conclusion: human neonates do not satisfy the Self-Consciousness Requirement and therefore do not possess a serious Right to Life. Thus, infanticide during the first few weeks of life does not violate a person's right to life.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect:** Tooley explicitly attacks biological genetic reductionism, branding it an ungrounded prejudice.\n- **Option C is incorrect:** It reduces a sophisticated psychological criteria of personhood to a biological reflex (pulmonary respiration).\n- **Option D is incorrect:** Tooley is an analytical secular philosopher whose work is founded on moral psychology, not religious dogma.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Michael Tooley, \"Abortion and Infanticide,\" *Philosophy & Public Affairs*, Vol. 2, No. 1 (1972), pp. 37–65.\n\n---",
      "reference": ""
