      "title": "Doctrinal Matching — Key Arguments in Applied Ethics)",
      "format_type": "D",
      "question": "Match the celebrated applied ethics thought experiment / argument in **List-I** with its author and philosophical function in **List-II**:\n\n| List-I (Argument / Scenario) | List-II (Author & Philosophical Function) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **p. The Expanding Child in the Tiny House** | **1.** Demonstrates that being a \"teleological center of life\" pursuing its own good gives all living organisms inherent worth. (Paul Taylor) |\n| **q. Smith and Jones at the Bathtub** | **2.** Illustrates that a mother has the right to defend her life against an innocent threat when continuing pregnancy threatens her survival. (Judith Jarvis Thomson) |\n| **r. Biocentric Egalitarianism & Teleological Centers** | **3.** Demonstrates that what makes killing wrong is the loss of a valuable future of experiences, rather than personhood. (Don Marquis) |\n| **s. The Future-Like-Ours (FLO) Principle** | **4.** Proves that there is no intrinsic moral difference between actively killing and passively letting die. (James Rachels) |\n\nChoose the correct matching code:",
      "options": {
        "A": "p-2, q-4, r-1, s-3",
        "B": "p-4, q-2, r-3, s-1",
        "C": "p-2, q-1, r-4, s-3",
        "D": "p-3, q-4, r-1, s-2"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A",
      "grounding": "- **p matches 2 (Thomson):** In \"A Defense of Abortion\" (§2), Thomson imagines being trapped inside a tiny house with a rapidly growing child. If you do nothing, the child will crush you to death against the walls. The child is innocent, but it is an innocent threat to your life. Thomson uses this to prove that third parties or the mother herself have the moral right to defend the mother’s life through abortion when pregnancy is life-threatening.\n- **q matches 4 (Rachels):** In \"Active and Passive Euthanasia\" (1975), Rachels introduces Smith drowning the cousin and Jones letting the cousin drown to demonstrate that the bare distinction between killing and letting die carries no moral difference.\n- **r matches 1 (Paul Taylor):** In *Respect for Nature: A Theory of Environmental Ethics* (1986), Paul Taylor establishes biocentric egalitarianism: every individual organism (plant, animal) is a \"teleological center of life\" that has a good of its own.\n- **s matches 3 (Don Marquis):** In \"Why Abortion is Immoral\" (1989), Marquis derives the wrongness of killing from the deprivation of a Future-Like-Ours (FLO).",
      "distractor_analysis": "All other options fail to align these canonical thought experiments with their exact philosophical creators and purposes. For example, Code B assigns the Smith and Jones case to Thomson, which is factually incorrect.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Thomson (1971); Rachels (1975); Taylor (1986); Marquis (1989).\n\n---",
