      "correct_option": "A",
      "correct_answer_text": "A",
      "grounding": "In his monumental concluding address to the Indian Constituent Assembly (November 25, 1949), Dr. B.R. Ambedkar delivered a chilling, prophetic warning:\n\n1. **The Life of Contradictions (Assertion A):**\n   \"On the 26th of January 1950, we are going to enter into a life of contradictions. In politics we will have equality and in social and economic life we will have inequality. In politics we will be recognizing the principle of one man one vote and one vote one value. In our social and economic life, we shall, by reason of our social and economic structure, continue to deny the principle of one man one value.\"\n2. **The Peril of Neglecting Social Democracy (Reason R):**\n   Ambedkar asked: \"How long shall we continue to live this life of contradictions? How long shall we continue to deny equality in our social and economic life? If we continue to deny it for long, we will do so only by putting our political democracy in peril. We must remove this contradiction at the earliest possible moment or else those who suffer from inequality will blow up the structure of political democracy which this Assembly has so laboriously built up.\"\n3. **The Trinity of Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity:**\n   Ambedkar defined political democracy as vacuous without **Social Democracy**. He insisted that Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity must not be treated as separate items, but as a **triune synthesis**:\n   - Liberty without equality produces the supremacy of the few over the many.\n   - Equality without liberty kills individual initiative.\n   - Without Fraternity (which Ambedkar identified with *Maitri* and universal brotherhood), liberty and equality cannot become a natural course of things. Thus, (R) provides the exact textual words and philosophical explanation for (A).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option B is incorrect:** (R) directly explains (A); the contradiction of political equality versus social-economic inequality is the explicit definition of the \"life of contradictions.\"\n- **Option C is incorrect:** (R) is Ambedkar's exact, celebrated formulation.\n- **Option D is incorrect:** (A) is one of the most famous quotes in Indian constitutional history.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** B.R. Ambedkar, *Speech in the Constituent Assembly on the Adoption of the Constitution*, November 25, 1949, in *Constituent Assembly Debates*, Vol. XI.\n\n---",
