      "question": "In *The Life Divine* (1939/1940), Sri Aurobindo formulates his philosophy of **Integral Advaita** (*Purna Vedanta*). What is the ontological relationship between **Involution** (*Tirobhava*) and **Evolution** (*Avirbhava*), and what is the transformative role of the **Supermind** (*Vijnana*)?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Involution is Darwinian natural selection, while evolution is biological extinction; the Supermind is an artificial supercomputer developed by modern technology.",
        "B": "Evolution is only possible because there has been a prior **Involution**: the supreme consciousness (*Saccidananda*) deliberately involved and veiled itself step-by-step into Inconscient Matter; therefore, evolution is the gradual ascending self-manifestation and emergence of Spirit out of Matter; the **Supermind** (*Vijnana*) is the intermediate, dynamic Truth-Consciousness that bridges the transcendent Absolute and the finite cosmos, whose descent into earthly matter will transform mental humanity into the **Gnostic Being** (*divine life on earth*).",
        "C": "Aurobindo adopts Sankara’s absolute Vivartavada, arguing that matter is an unreality created by Maya, and that the Supermind is a delusion that dissolves upon solitary individual Moksha.",
        "D": "Aurobindo argues that evolution is purely physical, and that the spirit never descends into matter."
      },
      "correct_option": "B",
      "correct_answer_text": "B",
      "grounding": "In *The Life Divine* (Vol. 1, Chapters 14–16 & Vol. 2, Chapters 26–28), Sri Aurobindo synthesizes the ancient Upanisadic vision with evolutionary theory:\n\n1. **The Law of Involution and Evolution:**\n   - How can consciousness emerge from inanimate matter? Modern materialist science is baffled by this.\n   - Aurobindo provides the metaphysical answer: \"Evolution is an inverse revelation of that which has involved itself.\" Spirit cannot evolve out of matter unless Spirit was already secretly *involved* in matter!\n   - **Involution:** The transcendent *Saccidananda* (Sat-Cit-Ananda: Existence-Consciousness-Bliss) plunged downwards through a series of veils: from Supermind, to Overmind, to Mind, to Life, down into the absolute density of the **Inconscient** (*Inconscience* / Matter).\n   - **Evolution:** The historical cosmos is the awakening and ascending return. Matter awakens into Life (plants/animals); Life awakens into Mind (humanity).\n2. **The Problem of the Human Mind:** Mind is not the summit of evolution! Mind is an organ of division, ignorance, and analytical fragmentation.\n3. **The Supermind (*Vijnana* / Truth-Consciousness):**\n   - The Supermind is the sovereign intermediary divine gnosis that possesses the unified vision of the One and the Many simultaneously.\n   - Unlike traditional Advaitic asceticism (Sankara), which sought an individual escape (*Moksha*) from an illusory world into Nirguna Brahman, Aurobindo’s goal is the **Spiritualization of Matter**—the descent (*Avatarana*) of the Supramental force into the earth's physical cells.\n   - This supramental descent transforms the ignorant human species into a community of **Gnostic Beings**, actualizing \"The Life Divine\" on earth.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **Option A is incorrect:** It reduces Aurobindo’s spiritual metaphysics to mechanical Darwinism and cybernetics.\n- **Option C is incorrect:** Aurobindo explicitly and repeatedly criticizes Sankara's doctrine of *Maya* as an \"illusionist compromise\" that cuts the world away from Brahman. For Aurobindo, the world is real (*Satyam*), a real manifestation of Brahman (*Parinama* of Divine Will).\n- **Option D is incorrect:** It denies involution and supramental descent, which are the twin core pillars of Aurobindo’s Integral Yoga.\n\n**Primary Source / Textual Reference:** Sri Aurobindo, *The Life Divine* (Pondicherry: Sri Aurobindo Ashram, 1939/1940), Book I, Chapters 14–16 (\"The Supermind as Creator\") and Book II, Part II, Chapters 27–28 (\"The Gnostic Being\").\n\n---",
      "reference": ""
