          "correct_option": "B",
          "reference": "Apadeva, *Mimamsa-Nyaya-Prakasa*; Laugaksi Bhaskara, *Arthasamgraha*.",
          "grounding": "Mimamsa classifies injunctions (*vidhis*) into three main types:\n  1. *Apurva-vidhi*: An injunction that enjoins something completely unknown through any other source (e.g., *svargakamo yajeta* - \"He who desires heaven should sacrifice\").\n  2. *Niyama-vidhi*: A restrictive injunction. When an action can be accomplished by more than one means (e.g., grains can be de-husked either by pounding with a mortar-pestle or by peeling with fingernails), the injunction *vrihin avahanti* (\"One pounds the paddy\") restricts the sacred rite to the method of pounding alone.\n  3. *Parisankhya-vidhi*: An exclusive injunction where two alternatives are simultaneously suggested by human desire, and the scripture excludes one to forbid it (e.g., \"Five five-nailed animals may be eaten\").\n  Therefore, \"One pounds the paddy\" is the standard textbook example of a *Niyama-vidhi*.",
