          "grounding": "Post-Sankara Advaita developed two primary sub-schools:\n  1. *Bhamati school*: Vacaspati Misra argues that Avidya cannot reside in Brahman, because Brahman is pure knowledge; nor can Brahman be subject to ignorance. Therefore, the individual soul (*Jiva*) is the locus (*asraya*) of Avidya, while Brahman is the content/object (*visaya*) of Avidya. Vacaspati defends this against circularity by positing a beginningless series of Jivas and Avidyas (*anadi-parampara*).\n  2. *Vivarana school*: Prakasatman argues that the Jiva is itself a product of Avidya; hence, it cannot be its locus. Brahman alone, as pure undifferentiated consciousness, is both the locus (*asraya*) and the object (*visaya*) of Avidya (*asrayatva-visayatva-bhaginyekacitireva*).\n  Therefore, both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) precisely captures this fundamental post-Sankara debate.",
