          "grounding": "In the *Theaetetus*, after refuting knowledge as perception (151d–186e) and knowledge as true judgment (187a–201c), Socrates investigates the third definition: knowledge is true judgment accompanied by an account (*doxa alethes meta logou*). Socrates analyzes three possible meanings of *logos*:\n  1. Making one's thought clear in speech through vocal sound (refuted: anyone who is not deaf or mute can do this without knowing).\n  2. An enumeration of the constituent elements of a thing (refuted by the \"Dream of Socrates\": elements themselves must be knowable, but if elements are simple, they have no account, making the complex composed of unknowable elements).\n  3. Stating the differentiating mark (*diaphora*) that distinguishes the object from all other things (refuted: to state the differentiating mark correctly, one must already *know* that mark, which makes the definition circular: \"knowledge is true belief accompanied by *knowledge* of the differentiating mark\").\n  Therefore, both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) directly provides Socrates' destructive argument.",
