          "grounding": "In Vlastos's celebrated logical analysis of the Third Man Argument:\n  1. *One-over-Many (OM)*: If a set of particulars $a, b, c$ are all $F$, there is a Form, $F$-ness, in virtue of which they are $F$.\n  2. *Self-Predication (SP)*: The Form of $F$-ness is itself $F$ (e.g., Largeness is large; Manhood is a man).\n  3. *Non-Identity (NI)*: If $x$ is $F$, $x$ cannot be identical with the Form of $F$-ness in virtue of which it is $F$.\n  From these three premises, the regress follows inevitably: Since particulars $a, b, c$ and the Form $F_1$ are all $F$ (by SP), there must be a second Form $F_2$ over them (by OM), which cannot be identical with $F_1$ (by NI). But then $a, b, c, F_1, F_2$ are all $F$, generating $F_3$, *ad infinitum*.\n  Statement IV (Recollection) is a Platonic doctrine from *Meno* and *Phaedo*, but it plays no role in generating the Third Man regress.\n  Hence, Statements I, II, and III only are the essential premises.",
          "distractor_analysis": "- (A), (C), and (D) either omit Non-Identity (without which $F_1$ could just participate in itself without a new Form) or erroneously include the irrelevant Recollection thesis.\n\n---"
