          "grounding": "Agrippa's Five Modes are: (1) Disagreement (*diaphonia*), (2) Infinite Regress (*eis apeiron ekballon*), (3) Relativity (*pros ti*), (4) Hypothesis (*hypothesis*), and (5) Circularity / Reciprocal Mode (*diallelos*).\n  When epistemologists attempt to justify any claim $P$:\n  - If they cite reason $Q$, they must justify $Q$ by $R$, leading to **Infinite Regress**.\n  - If they stop at an unproven axiom, it is a **Dogmatic Arbitrary Assumption (Hypothesis)**.\n  - If they use $P$ to justify $Q$ and $Q$ to justify $P$, they commit **Vicious Circularity**.\n  This triad—Regress, Dogmatism, and Circularity—constitutes the famous \"Agrippan Trilemma\" (later revived by Jakob Fries and Hans Albert as the *Münchhausen Trilemma*), showing that foundational justification of knowledge is structurally impossible.",
          "distractor_analysis": "- *(A) is erroneous*: Disagreement and Relativity are the first and third modes of Agrippa, but do not form the specific Trilemma of justification.\n  - *(C) is erroneous*: These are Descartes' skeptical arguments in the *First Meditation*.\n  - *(D) is erroneous*: These are 20th-century philosophy of science terms.\n\n---"
