          "format_type": "Assertion-Reason",
          "question": "- **Assertion (A)**: In St. Augustine's *Confessions* (Book XI), he solves the ontological problem of time—namely, that past is no more, future is not yet, and present has no duration—by asserting that time is an objective, physical, astronomical movement of celestial spheres.\n- **Reason (R)**: Augustine conceptualizes time as a psychological distension of the mind (*distentio animi*), wherein the mind simultaneously remembers the past (memory), attends to the present (direct perception), and anticipates the future (expectation).\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer:",
          "options": {
            "A": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).",
            "B": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).",
            "C": "(A) is false, but (R) is true.",
            "D": "(A) is true, but (R) is false.\n\n---"
          },
          "correct_option": "C",
          "reference": "St. Augustine, *Confessions*, Book XI, Chapters 14–28.",
          "grounding": "In Book XI of *Confessions*, Augustine famously asks: \"What then is time? If no one asks me, I know; if I wish to explain it to one who asks, I know not.\" Augustine explicitly **rejects** the view that time is the movement of celestial bodies (the sun, moon, and stars), pointing out that if the sun stopped moving but a potter's wheel kept spinning, time would still elapse.\n  Therefore, Assertion (A) is completely **FALSE**.\n  Augustine's genuine solution is that time is a subjective distension of the soul/mind (*distentio animi*): the past exists as present memory (*memoria*), the present as direct attention (*contuitus*), and the future as present expectation (*expectatio*). Thus, (R) is true. Hence, (C) is the correct option: (A) is false, but (R) is true.",
          "distractor_analysis": "- (A), (B), and (D) are incorrect because they fail to identify that Assertion (A) directly contradicts Augustine's explicit rejection of astronomical theories of time in Chapter 23 of Book XI.\n\n---"
