          "grounding": "- Statement I is correct: Anselm's *Proslogion* II defines God as *aliquid quo nihil maius cogitari possit* and argues that existing in reality (*in re*) is greater than existing merely in the understanding (*in intellectu*).\n  - Statement II is correct: Gaunilo constructed his *reductio ad absurdum* by substituting the \"Lost Island\" (*insula perdita*) blessed with all riches, arguing that by Anselm's logic, this island must exist in reality, which is absurd.\n  - Statement III is FALSE: Anselm did not accept that the Lost Island exists! In his reply, Anselm argued that Gaunilo's analogy fails fundamentally because an island is a contingent, material thing whose essence does not involve existence.\n  - Statement IV is correct: In *Proslogion* III and his *Reply to Gaunilo*, Anselm emphasizes that God uniquely possesses **necessary existence**; only of God is it impossible to conceive that He does not exist. An island can always be conceived not to exist.\n  Therefore, Statements I, II, and IV are correct.",
          "distractor_analysis": "- (A), (C), and (D) are incorrect because they either accept the false Statement III or omit Statements I, II, or IV.\n\n---"
