          "grounding": "For Aquinas, the fundamental distinction in metaphysics is between Essence (*essentia*—what a thing is) and Existence (*esse*—the act of being).\n  In created beings (including angels and material humans), essence and existence are really distinct (*distinctio realis*). A creature's essence is a potentiality that receives existence as an act from God; no creature exists by virtue of its own nature.\n  In God alone, there is no composition of essence and existence. God's essence is His existence (*Deus est suum esse*); He is Self-Subsistent Being Itself (*Ipsum Esse Subsistens*). If essence and existence were distinct in God, God would be a caused, contingent composite.",
