          "question": "Match the medieval positions on the Problem of Universals in List-I with their foundational philosophical proponents and definitions in List-II:\n\n| List-I (Doctrinal Position) | List-II (Proponent & Definition) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| a. Exaggerated / Extreme Realism | 1. William of Ockham: Universals exist merely as mental names or concepts (*conceptus/nomina*), having no ontological status in extramental reality. |\n| b. Moderate Realism | 2. Peter Abelard: Universals are neither real things nor mere physical vocal sounds (*flatus vocis*), but meaningful predicates (*sermones/nomina*). |\n| c. Conceptualism / Non-realism | 3. Thomas Aquinas: Universals exist *in re* (individualized in particulars), *post rem* (abstracted by the intellect), and *ante rem* (as divine ideas). |\n| d. Nominalism | 4. William of Champeaux: The universal essence is wholly, substantially identical in every individual of the species. |\n\nChoose the correct option:",
