          "question": "Consider the following statements regarding David Hume's epistemological critique of Causation in the *Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding*:\n- **Statement I**: According to \"Hume's Fork\", all objects of human reason divide into Relations of Ideas (demonstratively certain, discoverable by mere operation of thought) and Matters of Fact (known *a posteriori*, whose contrary is always possible).\n- **Statement II**: Hume argues that causal relations can never be discovered *a priori* by reason alone, but are known entirely from experience.\n- **Statement III**: Upon analyzing any single causal sequence (e.g., one billiard ball striking another), Hume discovers an observable impression of \"Necessary Connection\" (*necessitas*) directly inhering between the objects.\n- **Statement IV**: The idea of causal necessity is not an objective quality in objects, but a subjective feeling of determination or mental transition in the mind produced by constant conjunction and psychological custom/habit.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly correct?",
