          "grounding": "- Statement I is correct: Hume's Fork divides all knowledge into Relations of Ideas (geometry, algebra, logic—demonstratively certain) and Matters of Fact (empirical existence—contingent).\n  - Statement II is correct: Hume proves that cause and effect cannot be deduced *a priori* (Adam, newly created, could not tell from the transparency of water that it would drown him, nor from fire that it would burn him).\n  - Statement III is FALSE: Hume repeatedly searches single instances of causation (e.g., one billiard ball striking another) and discovers only: (1) Contiguity in space, (2) Priority in time, and (3) Constant Conjunction. He finds **no observable impression of necessary connection** whatsoever in the physical objects!\n  - Statement IV is correct: The idea of necessary connection arises as an internal impression of reflection—a custom-induced mental determination or feeling of transition in the observer's mind.\n  Therefore, Statements I, II, and IV are correct.",
