          "reference": "Douglas Walton, *Informal Logic: A Pragmatic Approach*; Copi, *Introduction to Logic*.",
          "grounding": "- Statement I is correct: Inferring the antecedent from the consequent ($P \\to Q, Q \\vdash P$) is the formal fallacy of Affirming the Consequent.\n  - Statement II is FALSE: Inferring that the whole has a property because each of its parts has that property is the fallacy of **Composition** (not Division). Division is the reverse inference from the properties of the whole to the properties of the parts.\n  - Statement III is correct: Inferring that a claim is true merely because it has not been proven false is an Appeal to Ignorance (*Argumentum ad Ignorantiam*).\n  - Statement IV is correct: Asserting without adequate causal warrant that an initial step will inevitably lead to an extreme catastrophe is a Slippery Slope fallacy.\n  Therefore, Statements I, III, and IV are correct.",
