          "correct_option": "B",
          "reference": "Jacques Derrida, *Of Grammatology* (1967); *Writing and Difference* (1967).",
          "grounding": "- Statement I is correct: Derrida deconstructs the traditional Western preference for spoken language over written language, demonstrating that Western philosophy has operated under the illusion of a \"Metaphysics of Presence\" (Logocentrism).\n  - Statement II is correct: The neologism *différance* intentionally blends the two senses of the French verb *différer*—to differ spatially and to defer temporally—showing that meaning is never fully present in a sign, but endlessly deferred through systemic differences.\n  - Statement III is FALSE: The phrase *\"Il n'y a pas de hors-texte\"* does NOT mean that the physical external world is non-existent. Derrida explicitly clarified that it means \"there is no outside-context\"—there is no pure, unmediated, absolute transcendental reality accessible outside of interpretative, contextual differential signs.\n  - Statement IV is correct: The concept of the *Trace* signifies that each linguistic sign carries within itself the retention of what it is not, undermining any pure, self-sufficient origin.\n  Therefore, Statements I, II, and IV are correct.",
