          "question": "Consider the following statements regarding H.P. Grice's theory of Conversational Implicature in \"Logic and Conversation\" (1975):\n- **Statement I**: Grice formulates the overarching \"Cooperative Principle\": \"Make your conversational contribution such as is required, at the stage at which it occurs, by the accepted purpose or direction of the talk exchange.\"\n- **Statement II**: The Cooperative Principle is articulated through four conversational Maxims: Quantity (informativeness), Quality (truthfulness), Relation (relevance), and Manner (clarity).\n- **Statement III**: A conversational implicature is generated when a speaker either straightforwardly observes a maxim or blatantly \"flouts\" (violates) a maxim with the expectation that the hearer will deduce the unstated pragmatic meaning.\n- **Statement IV**: Grice argues that conversational implicatures are identical to formal logical entailments and cannot be cancelled.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are strictly correct?",
